 will be disaggregated by age, gender and \n(in some instances) disability \nEfficiency:  Population \nProvinces) \nreturn/repatriation of children in India  \n\nin  the  territories  (countries,  States, \nsafe \n\ncovered  by  mechanisms \n\nfor  voluntary \n\nFinal \n\nMOVs \nPopulation source; UNICEF reporting; MoU between the source, \ntransit and destination States; SOPs manual on coordination and \ninformation sharing for India, Bangladesh and Nepal \nDisaggregation \nThe data will be disaggregated by age and gender \nEfficiency:  Child  population  in  targeted  areas  covered  by  case \nmanagement and referral system in Pakistan \n\nMOVs \nPopulation data source will be the census; UNICEF reporting; The \nSOPs  which  will  be  developed  to  functionalise  the  case \nmanagement and referral system \nDisaggregation  \nEffectiveness: Increase in the number of child protection structures \nand mechanisms that support and track case management of child \nlabour cases, of children with disabilities or children on the move \nMOVs \nUNICEF reporting \nDisaggregation  \n\nConsider the estimated % of child population at \nrisk  of  trafficking/migration  instead  of  the \nentire  population,  and  add  disaggregation  by \ndisability status; specify why it is not possible to \ncollect  admin  data  on  the  number  of  children \nbenefitting from mechanisms (reference to the \nrisk matrix)  \nSpecify  the  population  source  and  eliminate \nother not relevant MOVs \nAdd  to  the  disaggregation  disability  status  if \npossible \n\nConsider  the  estimated  %  of  child  population \nwith  protection  needs,  rather  than  the  entire \npopulation,  and  add  disaggregation  by \ndisability status.  \nAlternatively, consider the number of children \ncovered by case management, as reported by \nthe IMS put in place – in this case, the cost per \nchild would sensibly increase, but it would still \nbe a fully justifiable and acceptable investment \nSpecify  the  MOV  (Year  of  the  Census);  if \noutdated, \nidentify  an  alternative  MoV; \neliminate other not relevant MoVs \nSpecify the disaggregation by age, gender and \ndisability status if possible \n\nRevise MOVs to include official sources \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 31 OF 62 \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\x0c5. Outline for an Evaluation61  \nTwo research questions are related to the stakeholders’ demand for and use of a final evaluation.62 \nConsidering the initial stage of the programme, and the composite nature of its interventions,  very \nlimited information has been collected on the demand for an evaluation. \nWhile  some  respondents  underlined  the  need  to  consider  all  the  aspects  of  the  programme \ninterventions,  a  specific  interest  was  expressed  in  the  evaluation  of  the  translation  of  strategic \napproaches and analytical findings into concrete service delivery – i.e. the degree of comprehension \nof the new evidence, and of engagement in devising more effective ways to contrast child labour, of \ncounterparts and public service providers. \n\n5.1 Approach \n\nConsidering the suggested refinement of the TOC coherently with the programme results structure, it \nwould  be  technically  possible  to  conduct  an  impact  or  outcome  level  evaluation.  However,  the \nmultiple interventions developed in parallel, and the application of strategic approaches that could \nsustain promising, innovative solutions, seems to suggest that these wouldn’t be the most indicated \ntypes of evaluation for this programme, since it would not focus on key learning elements on how the \nmanagement of new knowledge on specific vulnerabilities related to child labour, and the support to \na CP systemic approach, can be instrumental in promoting the multiplication of practices that would \neventually reduce child labour and economic exploitation.  \nA  learning-focused  process  evaluation  could  be  a  relevant  exercise,  attempting  to  determine  how \nsuccessfully  the  programme  followed  the  strategy  laid  out  in  the  logic  model.  This would  allow  to \nmake an important distinction between implementation and theory, and to understand, in case of lack \nof results, whether these are related to implementation practices, or to the fact that the theory that \nlinked  activities  to  expected  outcomes  is  incorrect.63  This  would  help  to  pinpoint  strengths  and \nweaknesses within the programme design, and identify improvements for future scale-up efforts.  \nA  process  evaluation  would  allow  to  focus  on  the  strategies  applied  to  the  programme \nimplementation – and documented in the TOC – also in consideration of the fact that, according to the \nprogramme  timeline,  the  three  outcomes  will  be  pursued  in  parallel,  and  it  will  not  be  possible  to \nestablish a linear sequencing of results. 64  \nDepending on the timing of the evaluation, which doesn’t necessarily need to be conducted at the end \nof  the  programme,  a  process  evaluation  can  also  be  used  as  a  checkpoint  for  the  programme \nimplementation, ensuring that the programme is delivered according to design. In addition, a process \nevaluation  creates  a  feedback  loop  by  including  routine  assessments  such  as  documentation  of \nresources  used,  measurement  of  output  indicators,  and  tracking  of  programme  reach  among  the \ntarget  population.  The  knowledge  produced  would  in  this  case  inform  the  adaptive  learning  and \nmanagement that the programme intends to apply. \nIn the case of a process evaluation, indicators should be developed based on priorities collectively \nidentified by the stakeholders involved, while relating back to the original evaluation questions and \nthe  key  processes  highlighted  in  the  logic  model.  Therefore,  the  work  on  identifying  the  process \n\n61  The  considerations  included  in  this  chapter  have  been  formulated  in  consultation  with  UNICEF  ROSA \nEvaluation Unit, and with the contribution of Rose Meri Thompson Coon, Evaluation Specialist \n62 Cf. questions in Section for of the Evaluability matrix (Annex I)  \n63  All  the  considerations  on  process  evaluation  are  based  on  Unite  For  Sight,  Module  5:  Process  Evaluation. \nOverview and Benefits of Conducting a Process Evaluation, https://www.uniteforsight.org/evaluation-course \n64 Therefore, the evidence produced out of outcome 1 will be more relevant for other stakeholders, with whom \nit will be shared, than for the service providers and community members that will be involved in the achievement \nof outcomes 2 and 3 \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 32 OF 62 \n\n \n \n \n\x0cindicators and evaluation framework should start during the first/second implementation year.  \nAdaptive learning could also benefit from the application of a development evaluation (DE) approach, \nconsidering the strategic goals of the programme, and its composite design. This approach helps to \nidentify potentially promising practices or products and guide the strategic decision-making process \nthat comes with innovation.  While considering this option, it should be noted that  DE is not about \nproduct  or  service  improvement,  it’s  about  product  and  service development. It  involves  a  shift  in \nmindset  from  growth  and  ‘best  practices’  to  one  of  mindful,  strategic,  adaptative  strategy  and \ndevelopmental design.  \nDE in fact is not separate from strategy, but a critical part of it. There must be close ties between those \ndeveloping and implementing strategy and the evaluation team or evaluator. A bi-directional flow of \ninformation  is  required  through  regular,  ongoing  communications  so  that  strategy  informs  the \ndevelopment  evaluation,  and  this  informs  the  strategy  simultaneously.  This  would  probably  be  a \nchallenging  aspect  in  the  case  of  CLECSAP,  where  strategic  and  management  responsibilities  are \nshared  by  one  regional  and  two  country  offices,  with  different  perspectives  and  implementation \nmodalities.  \nDE is however worth considering since it helps programs innovate, learn, and adapt more fully, but \nthat isn’t always easy. A strong DE involves deep engagement with data, a commitment to learning, \nand a willingness to embrace (or at least accept) volatility, uncertainty, complexity, and ambiguity. \nThis requires changing the way organizations work and interact with their programs, which requires \ntime, energy, and sustained attention.65 \n\n5.2 Methodology \n\nAmong  the  possible  evaluation  methodologies  to  be  applied,  one  relevant  option  seems  to  be  a \ncontribution analysis, offering a step-by-step approach designed to help the stakeholders involved \narrive at conclusions about the contribution the programme has made (or is currently making) to the \nexpected outcomes. \nThis  methodology  can  be  applied  considering  that  the  CLECSAP  is  founded  on  a  relatively  clearly \narticulated TOC, and there is little scope for varying how the programme is implemented.  \nThe report from a contribution analysis would provide a line of reasoning from which it is possible to \ndraw  a  plausible  conclusion  that,  within  some  level  of  confidence,  the  programme  has  made  an \nimportant contribution to the documented results.66 \nIn consideration of the type of evidence produced by the programme indicators, which is more useful \nfor  ongoing  monitoring  exercises  than  for  an  evaluation;  and  also  in  consideration  of  the  type  of \nchanges  the  programme  is  expected  to  produce  –  on  the  awareness,  knowledge,  capacity, \nengagement of various stakeholders – outcome harvesting exercises could also be applied alongside \nthe  contribution  analysis.  The  methodology  would  help,  rather  than  measuring  progress  towards \npredetermined  outcomes,  to  collect  evidence  on  what  has  changed  and,  working  backwards,  to \ndetermine whether and how the programme interventions have contributed to these changes. The \n‘harvested’ outcomes could be positive or negative, intended or unintended, direct or indirect, but the \nconnection between the interventions and these outcomes should be plausible.67  \nThe suggested evaluative areas where the strategic approach of the programme would add value to \nthe work of policy makers and service providers, and overall to the CP evidence base in South Asia, \nare: \n\n▪  The production of new evidence on the implications of child labour for children with disabilities, \nand on effective service responses to child labour; and the contribution of the new knowledge on \n\n65 Cf. https://www.cense.ca/developmental-evaluation-a-short-introduction/ \n66Cf. https://www.betterevaluation.org/en/plan/approach/contribution_analysis#:~:text=Contribution%20 \nAnalysis%20is%20an%20approach,in%20real%2Dlife%20program%20evaluations. \n67 Cf. https://www.betterevaluation.org/en/plan/approach/outcome_harvesting \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 33 OF 62 \n\n \n \n \n\x0cthe formulation of policies and models of services \n\n▪  The  application  of  KM  and  advocacy  strategies  within  and  across  the  countries,  at  policy, \n\nservice delivery and inter-sectoral levels \n\n▪  The promotion of a systemic approach to child protection, focusing on case management and \nreferral mechanism, as the enabler of an integrated approach to child labour, involving duty \nbearers at various levels and facilitating multi-sectoral responses \n\n5.3 Recommendation \n\nGiven the programme intervention logic, the final evaluation design would most likely require a mixed-\nmethod  approach  using  a  rich  set  of  evaluation  methods  and  tools  to  capture  the  learning  and \naccountability elements in a balanced way.  \nIt  is  therefore  recommended  to  organise  an  evaluation  task-force,  under  the  leadership  of  the  RO \nEvaluation section, and involving evaluation focal points in the two COs, to decide the design of the \nCLECSAP  evaluation,  during  the  initial  stage  of  the  programme  implementation  (i.e.  by  the  end  of \n2020). This would allow to define well in advance the evaluation indicators and processes required by \nthe approach and methodologies selected, and define the most appropriate timing for the evaluation \nexercise – which would most likely not be the end of the programme. Technical expertise would need \nto be mobilized accordingly.  \n\n6. Conclusions \n\nThe CLECSAP EA exercise aimed at assessing first of all the clarity of the TOC, the results chain, and \ntheir  alignment  to  the  planned  programme  efforts  and  strategies.  This  was  done  by  testing  the \nprogramme  design,  and  the  evidence  produced  during  the  programme  inception  phase,  against \neleven inquiry parameters, that would indicate the evaluation readiness of the programme. The TOC \nand result logic provide sufficient evidence in relation to the programme relevance, expected impact, \ncontextualization,  efficiency  and  sustainability.  A  few  adjustments  would  be  required  to  make  the \nprogramme  fully  evaluable  from  the  point  of  view  of  clarity,  effectiveness,  coherence,  targeting, \nequity, and risks mitigation. \nThe adequacy and validity of the programme indicators, tools and systems for monitoring, measuring \nand verifying results were also assessed, through the analysis of five parameters. While information \nmanagement  criteria  seem  to  be  sufficiently  addressed,  some  adjustments  are  recommended  to \nensure the full adequacy, validity and completeness of indicators, and to provide evidence reflecting \nthe equity focused dimension of the results  \nDetailed recommendations have been provided to improve the weaknesses identified in the TOC, the \nresults  statements  and  the  indicators,  including  a  focus  on  the  strategic  approaches  that  would \nincrease the value of the interventions promoted. \nOverall, there are no fundamental reasons why the CLECSAP would not be evaluable, even more so if \nthe recommended adjustments are applied to the results vertical and horizontal logic. \nThe planned results are not unachievable or unrealistic, and on the contrary in various instances are \nquite  cautious  and  relatively  ambitious.  While  risks  and  assumptions  would  require  additional \nconsiderations, the overall comprehension of the  problem addressed, and the strategic capacity to \nprovide effective responses are visible. \nWhile  the  causal  coherence  is  evident  in  the  sequence  of  actions  and  results  described  in  the \nprogramme document, and outlined in the Logframe and Indicators Definition and Monitoring Guide, \na few adjustments are required to ensure the reflection of the same coherence in the TOC, where the \nproblem addressed needs to be better defined to show the solution pathway that links strategies, \noutputs and outcomes to the achievement of a long-term, sustainable result. \nConsidering  the  emphasis  put  on  the  systemic,  integrated  approach  promoted  through  the \nprogramme, and the “evaluative” nature of the programme itself, the new knowledge produced by \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 34 OF 62 \n\n \n \n\x0can evaluation will most likely contribute to enhancing the strategic thinking and adaptive learning of \nthe  child  protection  sector  in  responding  to  –  and  finally  contributing  to  the  eradication  of  –  the \neconomic exploitation of children, and specifically of particularly vulnerable groups. The CLECSAP can \nprovide  valuable  contributions  in  the  awareness  of  the  intrinsically  multi-sectoral  nature  of  the \nresponse required to contrast child labour.  \nIn this sense, a process evaluation linking the logic model underpinning the programme design to the \nachieved  outcomes  seems  to  be  the  most  interesting  approach  to  appreciate  the  value  of  the \nprogramme  interventions,  and  focus  on  modelling  and  learning  rather  than  on  net  impact  or \noutcomes; a contribution analysis, or outcome harvesting exercises, could provide relevant evidence \nfor the application of adaptive learning to the results achieved throughout the programme. \nThe programme is coherently linked to regional and national priorities defined by Governments and \nUN  Agencies  in  various strategic  documents,  and  contributes  to  the  achievement  of  goals  that  go \nbeyond its specific interventions; the formulated results will be achieved through the combination and \nenhancement of interventions promoted by UNICEF CP programmes in the region and in the countries \ninvolved. In this sense – especially for Pakistan – the results achieved will not be entirely attributable \nto the CLECSAP/DFID funds, but would be the result of a complex set of interventions implemented \nby both UNICEF and its Government counterparts, to which the CLECSAP will contribute.  \nThe EA exercise, while providing  analytical inputs  for the  enhancement  of the  programme design  at  a \ntimely  stage,  has  also  attempted  to  stimulate  the  application  of  evaluative  thinking  among  the  key \nstakeholders involved (primarily UNICEF professionals, but also counterparts), and has hopefully helped \nthose who will have the responsibility of programme expected results to consider additional aspects that \nwould make the programme more evaluable.68 \nAs a final reflection, it seems opportune to raise a flag on the unpredicted changes that happened \nglobally during the last months: among the considerations shared during the in-depth interviews, the \nclear awareness emerged of the fact that the current situation determined by the CoVid19 pandemic \nwill  have  major implications  on  the  programme,  in particular  related  to  the worsening situation  of \nfamilies (and especially of those whose parents were migrant workers and returned to their country \nof origin without any livelihood perspective) and to the reduced capacity of Governments to allocate \nresources to social services, as a consequence of the economic recession.  \nThe  implications  for  the  engagement  of  children  in  child  labour  could  be  devastating;  the \nimplementation  of  CLECSAP  is  more  timely  than  ever,  and  would  hopefully  be  able  to  adapt  and \nrespond to the unpredicted changes that will necessarily have to be taken into consideration.69 \n\n68 As mentioned by a few stakeholders interviewed, who found the exercise an ‘eye-opener’ \n69 Including revising timelines and budget allocations, and assessing CoVId19-related specific risks \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 35 OF 62 \n\n \n \n \n \n \n\x0cAnnexes – Evaluability Assessment Tools \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 36 OF 62 \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\x0cI. Evaluability matrix \n\nEvaluability assessment \nobjectives \n\nInquiry \nparameter \n\nKey questions \n\nData collection methods \n\n1. \nAssess the clarity of \noutcomes, the TOC and the \nalignment of planned \nprogramme efforts and \nstrategies to the TOC – as well \nas to the regional and \ncountry context, and \nUNICEF’s Strategic Plan. \n\nIs  the  ToC \ndescribed? \n\nfor  CLECSAP  adequately \n\nClarity \n\nDo the outputs, outcomes and overall goal \nfollow a vertical logic?  \n\nAre the programme objectives relevant to \nthe needs of the target group, as identified \nby any form of situation analysis?  \n\nRelevance \n\nEffective-\nness \n\nAre  the  planned  programmatic  strategies \nand  interventions  conceptually  adequate \nto  meet  what  is  detailed  in  the  TOC  as \nneeded to