\n\nUNICEF ROSA \n\nRobert Carr  \n\nChief, PMR  \n\nUNICEF Pakistan \n\nConsultations and \nupdates; \nconceptualization inputs \nValidation meeting  \nIn-depth interview \n\n25 June 2020 \n8, 20, 29, 30 July \n2020 \n\n29 June 2020 \n\nKurram Jilani \n\nConsultant, social \npolicy and research \n\nUNICEF Pakistan \n\nIn-depth interview  \n\n29 June 2020 \n\nShakeel Ahmed \n\nCP KM Officer \n\nUNICEF Pakistan \n\nIn-depth interview \n\n01 July 2020 \n\nRohit Raj Singh \n\nAssistant Labour \nCommissioner  \n\nDepartment of Labour, Bihar \nState, India \n\nIn-depth interview \n\n10 July 2020 \n\nNamita Shekhar \n\nChild Welfare Officer \n\nSaif Ur Rahman \n\nUNICEF Consultant \n\nSaeed Ahmad \n\nDirector General \n\nNasir Ali Baloch \n\nDeputy Director Child \nProtection – CPCMRS \nfocal point \n\nChild Welfare Committee, \nPatna District, Bihar State, \nIndia \nDepartments of Labour \nSocial Welfare, Bihar State, \nIndia \nLabour Department, \nBalochistan Province, \nPakistan \nDepartment of Social \nWelfare, Human Rights and \nSpecial Education \nBalochistan Province, \n\nIn-depth interview \n\n10 July 2020 \n\nIn-depth interview \n\n10 July 2020 \n\nIn-depth interview \n\n16 July 2020 \n\nIn-depth interview \n\n17 July 2020 \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 50 OF 62 \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\x0cPakistan  \n\nMicaela Pasini \n\nChief CP \n\nUNICEF Pakistan \n\nValidation meeting \n\n30 July 2020 \n\nMuhammad Zahoor \n\nC4D Officer, CP \nsection \n\nUNICEF Pakistan \n\nValidation meeting \n\n30 July 2020 \n\nNirmala Pandey \n\nCP Specialist  \n\nUNICEF India  \n\nValidation meeting \n\n30 July 2020 \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 51 OF 62 \n\n \n \n \n \n \n\x0cVI. CLECSAP Indicator definition and monitoring guide  \n\nCHILD LABOUR: EXPLOITATION OF CHILDREN IN SOUTH ASIA \nINDICATOR DEFINITION AND MONITORING GUIDE \nMarch 2020 \n\nIndicator \n\nKey Definition \n\nMethod of Computation \n\nSource/Means of \nVerification \n\nLevel of Disaggregation \n\nAdditional \nInformation \n\nOutcome 1: Government and partners within and across countries support the generation and use of robust data and evidence to inform policy and programming, track progress, and document lessons on the \neconomic exploitation of children/child labour, including children with disabilities and children on the move  \n\nNot Applicable \n\nOne  policy  +  one  evidence  =  one \ndocument \n\nUNICEF \nreporting, \nverified  with  copy  of \nthe \npolicy \ndocument(s) \n\nType of document: \n\nLaws \n\nAnd \n\nRegulations/standards \n\nSource  of  evidence \nthat  is  used  for  the \ndevelopment of policy \ndocument(s) \n\nStandard \nProcedures (SOPs) \n\nOperating \n\nPolicies, including white \npapers \n\nAnd \n\nStrategies \n\nA  record  of  support \nprovided by UNICEF \n\nAction Plans \n\nOutcome Indicator 1: \n\nNumber  of  government \npolicy  documents \nthat \nreference  and/or  build \nupon \ncurrent \ndata/evidence \n\nIndia \n\n(baseline: 0; target: 3) \n\nPakistan \n\n(baseline: 0; target: 1) \n\nThis is a numeric (number) indicator and one which UNICEF will \ncontribute to, at the national and/or State/Provincial level, as \nappropriate. \n\nThe  indicator  will  include  any  Government  document  (e.  g. \nLaws,  Regulations,  Policies,  Strategies,  Standard  Operating \nProcedures,  Action  Plans)  which  has  been  developed  and/or \nrevised during the project period with UNICEF support and/or \nengagement.   \n\nThe baseline and target for this indicator will only include policy \ndocuments  from  the  States/districts  and/or  Province  where \nUNICEF is implementing the child labour programme. \n\nUNICEF support can refer to technical and/or financial support, \nincluding the sharing of data, studies, reports and/or analysis.   \n\nUNICEF’s engagement can be defined as any advocacy efforts \nby  UNICEF  which  contributes  to  the  development/revision  of \nthe documents.    \n\nIn addition, if evidence generated with UNICEF’s support is used \nin  the  development  and/or  revision  of  the  Government \ndocument(s), this can also be seen as a contribution. \n\nThe policy document(s) should have a focus on/address issues \nrelated to economic exploitation of children/child labour.   \n\nThe policy document(s) should be evidence based and be in line \nwith international standards.   \n\nChildren on the move are defined as those who have voluntarily \nor  involuntarily  moved,  within  or  between  countries,  with  or \nwithout  their  parents  or  primary  caregiver  and  whose \nmovement  might  place  them  at  risk  or  at  increased  risk  of \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 52 OF 62 \n\n \n \n \n \n  \n \n \n \n  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\x0cIndicator \n\nKey Definition \n\nMethod of Computation \n\nSource/Means of \nVerification \n\nLevel of Disaggregation \n\nAdditional \nInformation \n\neconomic  exploitation,  sexual  exploitation,  abuse,  neglect \nand/or violence (TdH 2019). \n\nChildren  with  disabilities  are  those  who  have  long-term \nphysical,  mental,  intellectual  and/or  sensory  impairments \nwhich in interaction with various barriers may hinder their full \nand  effective  participation  in  society  on  an  equal  basis  with \nothers (UNCRPD 2006).   \n\nThe  impairment  may  be  something  which  the  child  was  born \nwith,  acquired  in  her/his  lifetime,  was  inflicted  intentionally \nduring her/his lifetime and/or acquired as a result of working as \na child.  The impairment maybe visible or invisible/hidden.      \n\nOutput 1.1: Evidence is generated and disseminated on child labour and children with disabilities, including children on the move  \n\nOutput Indicator 1.1: \n\nAnalytical \nreports \navailable,  disseminated \nand  discussed  with  key \nstakeholders  by  2023 \n(baseline: 0; target: 4) \n\nThis is a numeric (number) indicator, which UNICEF ROSA and \nthe two Country Offices will be responsible for delivering.   \n\nNot Applicable \n\nDisaggregation  between supported \nwith  project \nfunds,  and  not \nsupported  with  project  funds  and \npartially  supported  with  project \nfunds \n\nThe analytical reports can include any one of the following, if \nUNICEF  played  a  role  in  providing  technical  and/or  financial \nsupport in planning and finalizing the reports. These include but \nare not limited to: \n\na)  Regional studies, on the economic exploitation of \n\nchildren/child labour and links between child labour \nand children with disabilities as well as children on \nthe move by June 2020, December 2022 and \nSeptember 2023     \n\nb)  An evaluability assessment to determine the overall \nreadiness of the programme to be evaluated and a \nreview of the indicator framework to assess its \nfeasibility by June 2020. \n\nc)  A country level, in depth analysis of the link between \n\nchildren with disabilities and economic exploitation \nof children/child labour in Pakistan, based on the \nStatistical Information and Monitoring Programme \non Children (SIMPOC)/Child Labour Survey (CLS) and \nthe Multiple Indicator Cluster Survey (MICS) in \nPakistan by October 2021 (for quantitative analysis) \nand August 2022 (for qualitative analysis). \nd)  An in-depth review of existing interventions and \nState-specific responses to address the economic \nexploitation of children/child labour as well as child \ntrafficking and the effectiveness and relevance of \nthese responses and intervention packages in India \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 53 OF 62 \n\nreporting, \nUNICEF \nverified \nwith \nelectronic  copy  of  the \nreports. \n\ntype  of \nNote \nthe \ncirculation \ne.g. \nwebsite,  meetings, \ndissemination events \n\nType of document: \n\nResearch  reports  and \nassessments \n(both \nprimary  research  and \nsecondary analysis) \n\nLiterature reviews \n\nDiscussion papers \n\nIf possible the analytical \nreports at country level \nwill be disaggregated by \nage,  gender,  disability \nand State/Province. \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\x0cIndicator \n\nKey Definition \n\nMethod of Computation \n\nSource/Means of \nVerification \n\nLevel of Disaggregation \n\nAdditional \nInformation \n\nby November 2020 and a review of implementation \nby November 2022.   \n\ne)  The final evaluation report of the overall project to \nassess the achievement of results, document good \npractices and lessons learnt and identify \nopportunities for further planning and scaling up of \ninterventions to end child labour and meet the \nneeds of child labourers with disabilities and on the \nmove by June 2023. \n\nOutput 1.2. Technical assistance is provided to relevant stakeholders in South Asia to address the economic exploitation of children/child labour, including children with disabilities and children on the move \n\nOutput Indicator 1.2 \n\nNumber \ntechnical \nof \nassistance (virtual and in-\nperson)  provided  across \nthe  region  (baseline:  0; \ntarget: 12) \n\nThis is a numeric (number) indicator, which UNICEF ROSA will \nbe responsible for delivering.   \n\nThe technical assistance provided by ROSA, across the region, \ncan be either virtual and/or in person, and specific to addressing \nchild labour and child economic exploitation including project \nmanagement  related  actions  for  this  project.  They  will  be  a \ncombination of any one of the following: \n\na) \n\nStart-up meeting at the inception of the project with \nkey colleagues from both Country Offices and the \nengagement of other partners as appropriate, to \nensure a common understanding of the \naccountabilities, expected deliverables and results. \nb)  Country level visits by the Regional Advisor and/or \nthe Child Protection Specialist to provide technical \nassistance on a specific issue, attend meetings, \nengage with counterparts, monitor project progress \nand ensure quality assurance and reporting.  \nStructured webinars and calls with the Country \nOffices to provide technical assistance and discuss \nquarterly project progress, challenges and adaptive \napproaches. \n\nc) \n\nNot Applicable \n\nUNICEF reporting \n\nVirtual \n\nWorkshop/meeting \nreports and minutes \n\nIn-Person \n\nMission \nreports \n\nToRs \n\nand \n\nBy country \n\nRegional meetings \n\nUNICEF  ROSA  Country \nSupport database \n\nCountry meetings \n\nWhile  this  is  primarily \nthe  responsibility  of \nROSA, \nit  will  also \ninclude  the  technical \nassistance \nprovided \nthrough  the  meetings \nconvened  with  expert \ngroups  during \nthe \nproject period.  \n\nd)  Written feedback to the COs on documents, reports, \n\ne) \n\nknowledge products, etc. \nStructured reflection meetings at the regional, global \nand country level with academics, practitioners and \npolicy makers to review project progress and/or \ndiscuss innovative practices and scaling up options.  \n\nOutcome 2. Effective child protection structures and mechanisms are in place to prevent and respond to the economic exploitation of children/child labour, including children with disabilities and children on \nthe move \n\nOutcome Indicator 2.1 \n\nThis is a numeric (proportion) indicator and will be calculated at \nthe State and Provincial level.   \n\nThe  unit  of  measure  will  be \ncomputed as follows: \n\nOffice of Statistics and \nData of the Country \n\nBy  State  and  Province \nif \nand  by \n\ngender, \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 54 OF 62 \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\x0cIndicator \n\nKey Definition \n\nMethod of Computation \n\nSource/Means of \nVerification \n\nLevel of Disaggregation \n\nAdditional \nInformation \n\nNumber  of  social  service \nworkers per 100,000 child \npopulation  \n\nIndia \n\n               Bihar  UP   WB \n\nbaseline: 7         7       6 \n\ntarget:     8         8       7 \n\nPakistan \n\n(baseline: 53; target: 53) \n\nOutcome Indicator 2.2 \n\nNumber  of  States  (India) \nand  Provinces  (Pakistan) \nintegrated \nwith \ninformation management \nsystem  that  support  and \ntrack case management \n\nIndia \n\n(baseline: 1;  target: 3) \n\nThe social service workforce in child protection is defined as a \nvariety of workers ─ formal and informal, paid and unpaid, \nprofessional and paraprofessional, governmental and non-\ngovernmental ─ that make the social service system function \nand contribute to the care, support, and protection of \nchildren. At the micro level, members of the social service \nworkforce, e.g. child and youth care workers or social workers, \nprovide direct support to vulnerable children (UNICEF 2018).  \nFor the purpose of this programme, however, this indicator is \ndefined as the number of Government social service workers as \nidentified by the country, who provide social services.   \n\nSocial work encompasses identifying and supporting \nindividuals who have experienced violence, abuse and \nexploitation, and includes identification, case management, \ncounselling, referral and coordination of a range of critical \nservices in follow-up to cases of violence, abuse and \nexploitation (UNICEF SP Indicator Manual, 2019).  \nIt does not include allied workers, who are professionals, para \nprofessionals  and  volunteers  who  are  directly  linked  to  the \neducation, health or justice sectors unless their specific role is \nin the delivery of social services (e.g. a police officer staffing the \nwomen and child desks; a social worker dedicated to a school). \n\nEach  country  office  will  provide  a  list  the  titles  of  the \nGovernment social service workers for each State/Province by \nMay 2020. It is acknowledged that as the project progresses this \nlist may change. \n\nUp-to-date data for the denominator on the number of children \nunder 18 years of age in the country will be obtained from the \ncensus of India (2011) and Pakistan (2017).   \n\nThis  is  a  numeric  (number)  indicator,  which  UNICEF  Country \nOffices will contribute to, and will be at the State and Provincial \nlevel.     \n\nAn information management system (IMS) is a tool to support \nthe  organisation  of  information,  and  in  this  specific  instance, \norganises \nindividual  children  who  are \neconomically  exploited  and/or  in  child  labour  in  order  to \nmanage the delivery of necessary services. \n\ninformation  on \n\nThe  information  management  system  should  also  include \nmeans of case management, which means it should be used to \nmanage the delivery of necessary services.  \n\navailable  through  the \nGovernment records \n\n=  (Number  of  government  social \nof \nservice  workers/Population \nChildren \nthe \nState/Province)*(100,000) \n\nin \n\nAdministrative \nsources/ \nGovernment Records \n\ndata \nOfficial \n\nNumber \n\nof \nNumerator: \ngovernment  social  service  workers \nat the  State/Provincial level, in the \npast calendar year \n\nRelevant \nSurveys \n\nNational \n\nDenominator: \nchildren  under  18  years \nState/Province \n\nPopulation \n\nof \nin  the \n\nNot Applicable \n\nof \n\nthe \n\nReports \nInformation \nManagement  System \navailable \nthe \nrelevant Ministry \n\nat \n\nMonitoring  missions \non IMS use \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 55 OF 62 \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\x0cIndicator \n\nKey Definition \n\nMethod of Computation \n\nSource/Means of \nVerification \n\nLevel of Disaggregation \n\nAdditional \nInformation \n\nPakistan \n\n(baseline: 0;  target: 1) \n\nIn addition, for India, this system should also include data on \nmigration  and  trafficking  of  children,  with  a  focus  on  child \nlabour.    For  Pakistan,  the  system  should  include  children  in \nlabour who are affected by disability.   \n\nThe definition of integrated refers to the alignment of different \nsystem’s data components in such a way that allows different \nsystems to be connected confidentially to contribute towards \nshared  programme  goals  and  metrics,  promote  data \ncompatibility  and  prevent  duplicate  counting,  and  ensure \nreferrals.  \n\nA common system must be used across the Province/State.    \n\nThe system must allow for disaggregation by gender, age and \nability.  \n\nOutput 2.1. Government-led structures, inclusive of civil society, are strengthened to prevent and respond to the economic exploitation of children/child labour, including children with disabilities and children \non the move \n\nOutput indicator 2.1 \n\nNumber  of  States  (India) \nand  Provinces  (Pakistan) \nwith  a  costed  plan  of \naction  to  address  child \nlabour/the \neconomic \nexploitation of children \n\nIndia  \n\n(baseline: 1; target: 3)  \n\nPakistan \n\n(baseline: 0; target: 1) \n\nThis is a numeric (number) indicator, which the UNICEF Country \nOffices will be responsible for delivering.   \n\nThe definition for this indicator is the number of States (India) \nand Provinces (Pakistan) which have a costed plan of action to \nend child labour/the economic exploitation of children.   \n\nNot Applicable \n\nUNICEF reporting \n\nThe  following  questions  should  be \nresponded to: \n\nElectronic  copies  of \nthe  costed  plans  of \naction \n\nThe costed action plan should have, at a minimum: (i) policy and \ncore  areas  of  focus,  (ii)  an  operational  plan  with  a  detailed \nbudget cost (based on cost analysis and considering the current \npublic sector expenditure) required to carry out activities (iii) an \nM&E \nroles  and \ninclude \nresponsibilities,  including  a  coordination  mechanism  (v)  more \nthan one Ministry/sector involved in the implementation.   \n\nframework  and \n\n(iv)  detail \n\nUNICEF should provide technical and/or financial support to the \nrelevant Ministry or Sector at the State and/or Provincial level \nin  supporting  the  development  of  the  costed  plan  of  action, \ntogether with other relevant partners.  The costed action plan \nshould  be  approved  and  disseminated  by  the  State  and/or \nProvincial level authorities.  \n\nIs  the  costed  plan  of  action: \nDrafted? Finalized?  \n\nHow  many  of  the  components \nmentioned  in  the  definition  are \nincluded in the: \n\nDraft action plan? \n\nFinal action plan? \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 56 OF 62 \n\n• \n\nUNICEF  will  have  to \nplay  a  key  role \nin \nensuring  the  costed \nplans  of  action  meets \nminimum \ninternational \nstandards  of \nprotection.   \n\nchild \n\nof \nprocess \nOne \ndeveloping  a  costed \naction  plan, \nis  as \nfollows:   \n• \n\nBased on evidence \ngenerated, an action \nplan is drafted in a \nmulti-sectoral and \ninclusive manner, \ninvolving more than \none ministry. \nThe above action   \nplan is developed    \nand budget \nallocated/costed \nagainst the \nresponsibilities of \ndifferent ministries. \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\x0cIndicator \n\nKey Definition \n\nMethod of Computation \n\nSource/Means of \nVerification \n\nLevel of Disaggregation \n\nAdditional \nInformation \n\n• \n\n• \n\nDue to UNICEF’s \nsupport and   \nadvocacy activities, \nthe final costed   \naction plan is in   \nplace. \nA costed plan is \napproved, \ndisseminated and \nimplemented. \n  For India, the State of \nBihar  already  has  a \ncosted  action  plan \nwith \nfive \nall \ncomponents. \n  West \nBengal  has  three  of \nthe  above-mentioned \ncomponents  (i,  iv  and \nv)  and  Uttar  Pradesh \nhas two of the above-\nmentioned \ncomponents  (i  and  iv) \nin their action plans. \n\nOutput 2.2 Referral system is established to prevent and respond to the economic exploitation of children/child labour, including children with disabilities and children on the move \n\nOutput Indicator 2.2 \n\nNumber  of  districts \nin \nIndia  and  Pakistan  with \nfunctional \ncase \nmanagement and referral \nmechanisms  \n\nIndia \n\n(baseline: 0; target: 30) \n\nPakistan \n\n(baseline: 0; target: 1) \n\nThis is a numeric (number) indicator, which the UNICEF Country \nOffices will be responsible for delivering.   \n\nThe definition for this indicator is the availability of a functional \nmodel  of  child  protection  case  management  and  referral \nmechanism(s), which covers the entire district and can respond \nto cases of economic exploitation/child labour, child labourers \nwith disabilities and on the move.   \n\nFunctional means that it is operational and being used.  \n\nCase  management  is  a  collaborative  process  of  registration, \nassessment,  planning,  facilitation  and  referrals  to  services  to \nrespond to the needs of a child in need of protection and her or \nhis family, as far as possible (Oxford Policy Management 2019).  \n\nReferral is a process of formally connecting a child in need of \nprotection  or  her/his  family  to  a  specific  service  provider  to \naddress an identified service need.  It involves communication \nbetween at least two different service providers and is usually \n\nNot Applicable \n\nUNICEF reporting \n\nSOP manuals \n\nMonitoring visits \n\nAdmin data \n\nshould \nThe  model \ninclude \ndata \ndisaggregated  by  age, \ngender,  disability  and \nfocus area. \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 57 OF 62 \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\x0cIndicator \n\nKey Definition \n\nMethod of Computation \n\nSource/Means of \nVerification \n\nLevel of Disaggregation \n\nAdditional \nInformation \n\na result of the case management (Oxford Policy Management \n2019).  \n\nThe SOPs is a set of step-by-step instructions to help workers \ncarry out complex but routine operations.  SOPs aim to achieve \nefficiency,  quality  output  and  uniformity  of  performance \n(Oxford Policy Management 2019).   \n\nThis  functional  model  should  be  cross-sectoral  such  that  the \ncase management and referral mechanism promote access to a \nrange  of  relevant  services  such  as  social  services,  social \nprotection,  health,  education,  employment  services,  legal \nservices,  police  and  other  stakeholders  providing  child \nprotection services.  \n\nThe functional case management and referral mechanism will \nalso include SOPs which will be developed with technical and/or \nfinancial input from UNICEF.  \n\nA functional case management and referral mechanism is one \nwhich:  \na) \n\nIs used by at least one allied department providing \nchild protection services; \n\nb)  Has clear SOPs; \nc)  Has trained staff who are knowledgeable on how to \n\nuse the mechanism;  \n\nOutput 2.3. Mechanisms (intercountry and interstate) for voluntary safe return/repatriation of children engaged in labour, including those with disabilities are in place (India)  \n\nOutput Indicator 2.3 \n\nNumber of States in India \ninter-state \nwith \nmechanisms \nfor \nvoluntary/safe \nreturn/repatriation  \n\n(baseline: 0; target: 3) \n\nThis is a numeric (number) indicator, which the UNICEF Country \nOffice in India will be responsible for delivering.   \n\nNot Applicable \n\nThe definition for this indicator is the availability of an approved \nmechanism or structure to ensure the voluntary/safe return of \neconomically  exploited  children.  This  mechanism  needs  to \ninclude: \n\ni) \nii) \niii) \niv) \nv) \n\nFamily tracing  \nTransport \nFamily reunification \nReintegration services \nBest Interest Determination (BID)  \nThe  mechanism  or  structure  will  include  a  Memorandum  of \nUnderstanding  (MoU)  and/or  SOPs  on  how  economically \nexploited children and children on the move/trafficked can be \nsafely returned. \n\nThe  following  questions  should  be \nresponded to: \n\nIs the MoU and/or SOPs:  Drafted? \nFinalized? \n\nHow  many  of  the  components \nmentioned  in  the  definition  are \nincluded in the: \n\nDraft MoU/SOP? \n\nFinal MoU/SOP? \n\nThe  mechanism  should \ninclude  data  of  source, \ntransit  and  destination \nStates  and \ntype  of \nexploitation. \n\nMoU  between \nsource, \ndestination States \n\nthe \ntransit  and \n\nand/or \n\nSOP  manual \ncoordination \ninformation sharing \n\non \nand \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 58 OF 62 \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\x0cIndicator \n\nKey Definition \n\nMethod of Computation \n\nSource/Means of \nVerification \n\nLevel of Disaggregation \n\nAdditional \nInformation \n\nThe  mechanism  should  address \ntransferability  of \nentitlements of economically exploited children/child labourers \nas they cross interstate borders as appropriate.   \n\nthe \n\nIt  should  effectively  coordinate  the  voluntary  return  of  child \nlabourers to the source State and staff should be trained on the \nimplementation  of  the  mechanism  for  safe  return,  including \nfamily tracing, case management and BID.  \n\nOutcome 3: Community-based mechanisms are operational to prevent and respond to the economic exploitation of children/child labour, including children with disabilities and children on the move  \n\nUNICEF  reporting  of \nstrengthened \nand \ndeveloped \ncommunity-based \nmechanisms at district \nlevel \n\nNumber  and  type  of \ngroups, \ncommunity \nchild \nwhich  address \nlabour/economic \nexploitation of children, \nwith  the  membership \ndisaggregated \nby \ngender and age. \n\nOutcome Indicator 3: \n\nNumber  of  districts \nin \nIndia  and  Pakistan  that \ndevelop  and  implement \ncommunity-based models \nof care and reintegration \n\nIndia \n\n(baseline: 0; target: 30) \n\nPakistan \n\n(baseline: 0; target: 1) \n\nThis is a numeric (number) indicator and will be calculated at \nthe district level for each of the States and the Province.     \n\nNot Applicable \n\nEach  district \nshould  have  a \nminimum of two CBCPMs to qualify \nfor this indicator \n\nThe definition of this indicator is to strengthen existing and/or \ndevelop new community-based child protection mechanisms to \naddress  issues  of  economic  exploitation  of  children/child \nlabour.    \n\nCommunity-based child  protection mechanisms (CBCPMs) are \nfront line efforts to protect children from exploitation, abuse, \nviolence,  and  neglect  and  to  promote  children’s  well-being. \nCBCPMs are defined broadly to include all groups or networks \nat grassroots level that respond to and prevent issues of child \nprotection.  These  may  include  family  support,  peer  group \nsupport,  and  community  groups  such  as  women’s  groups, \nreligious  groups,  and  youth  groups,  as  well  as  traditional \ncommunity  processes, \nand   \nmechanisms initiated by NGOs (Wessels, 2011).  \n\ngovernment  mechanisms, \n\nA community-based model should, at a minimum:   \n\n• \n\nStrengthen natural and existing supports and \nsystems (family, peers).  \n\n•  Make use of community knowledge and capacities.  \n• \n• \n\nBe in line with the principle of ‘do no harm’ \nInclude lay and/or professional members  \n\nAnd for this indicator it must: \n\n• \n\n• \n\nReach child labourers and/or address the issue of \nchild labour \nInclude UNICEF support and/or contribution  \nUNICEF’s  contribution  could  be  technical  and/or  advocacy  to \nstrengthen  the  existing  community-based  models  of  care  or \nhelp communities establish models where such models do not \nexist.  \n\nOutput 3.1: Parents, children and community members are supported to access available services that provide alternatives to the economic exploitation of children/child labour, including children with disabilities \nand children on the move \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 59 OF 62 \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\x0cIndicator \n\nKey Definition \n\nMethod of Computation \n\nSource/Means of \nVerification \n\nLevel of Disaggregation \n\nAdditional \nInformation \n\nThis is a numeric (number) indicator, which the UNICEF Country \nOffice in Pakistan will be responsible for delivering.   \n\n# people reached per one message \nmodality \n\nOutput Indicator 3.1a: \n\nand \n\nwith \non \n\nNumber  of  community \nincluding \nmembers \nparents \nchildren \nkey \nreached \nmessages \nthe \neconomic  exploitation  of \nchildren/child \nin \nof \ntarget \n(Pakistan) \nBaluchistan \n(baseline: \ntarget \n113,000) \n\ndistricts \n\nlabour \n\n0 \n\nOutput indicator 3.1b: \n\nNumber  of  districts \nIndia  which \nthe \nstrategy package  \n\nin \nimplement \nmulti-pronged \n\n(baseline: 0; target: 30) \n\nis \n\nas \n\nthe \n\ntotal \n\ndefined \n\nindicator \n\nnumber \nThis \nof individuals (boys, girls, women, and men) above five years of \nage reached through UNICEF supported community dialogues, \nstreet  drama,  community  meetings  and/or  mass  media \n(traditional  and  social  media)  with  messaging  on  economic \nexploitation  of  children/child \nlabour  and  children  with \ndisabilities in target districts.    \nIndividuals \n‘reached’  refers  to  the  community  member \nreceiving at least one key message on economic exploitation of \nchildren/child  labour  and  child  labourers  with  disabilities, \nthrough  at  least  one  mode  of  communication,  with  UNICEF’s \ndirect support. \nMultiple counting of individuals (girls, boys, women and men) \nwithin  the  target  districts is  to  be  avoided  by  using  one \ncommunication modality in one engagement.  \nMessages  can  be  delivered  through  any  of  the  means \nmentioned above.   \nThis is a numeric (number) indicator, which the UNICEF Country \nOffice in India will be responsible for delivering. \n\nThis  indicator  is  defined  as  the  number  of  districts  which \nimplement the multi-pronged strategy package which provides \nsolutions for children at risk of or engaged in child labour and \neconomic exploitation.    \n\nThe  implementation  of  this  package  should  be  a  coordinated \neffort  engaging \nthe  district \nadministration, \nlocal  councils,  village  child  protection \ncommittees,  and  community-based  organizations  among \nothers.   \n\nseveral  actors, \n\nsuch  as \n\nImplementation of the package will be considered effective, if \nit includes any two of the following: \n\na)  Provides access to education (formal, catch up, \n\nschool enrolment, school supplies) for children who \nhave been economically exploited \n\nb)  Provides economic opportunities for families \nc) \n\nSupports adolescents and youth to become agents \nof change to end child labour  \n\nd)  Supports child welfare committees to develop \n\nchildcare plans for child labourers.   \n\n#/100,000  population  above  five \nyears of age in the targeted district \n\nPopulation data for the country will \nbe  obtained  from  the  census  of \nIndia (2011) and Pakistan (2017).   \n\nof \n\nUNICEF CO in Pakistan \nreporting \nkey \nmessages  developed \nand \ncommunity \nmembers reached \n\nCommunity  members \nto  be  disaggregated  by \nIf \ngender  and  age. \npossible,  also  by  visible \ndisability. \n\nEnd line survey results \nfrom Pakistan \n\nNumber  of  message \ndelivery mechanism in \nPakistan  and \ntotal \nnumber of initiatives \n\nNot Applicable  \n\nReview of the Strategy \nPackage  to  confirm  it \ncovers the areas noted \n\nNot Applicable  \n\nfrom \n\nReports \nthe \nUNICEF ICO on the use \nand \nimplementation \nof  the  multi-pronged \nstrategy  package  to \nprovide  solutions  for \nchildren at risk of child \nlabour  and  economic \nexploitation \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 60 OF 62 \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\x0cIndicator \n\nKey Definition \n\nMethod of Computation \n\nSource/Means of \nVerification \n\nLevel of Disaggregation \n\nAdditional \nInformation \n\ne)  Provides health services for children who are or \n\nwere engaged in child labour  \n\nValue for Money Indicators \n\nArea \n\nIndicator \n\nKey Definition \n\nEconomy  \n\nCost \nper \nresearch piece \n\nEfficiency  \n\nby \n\nin \nPopulation \nthe  territories \n(countries, \nStates, \nProvinces) \ncovered \nmechanisms \nfor  voluntary \nsafe \nreturn/repatri\nation \nchildren \nIndia \n\nof \nin \n\nThis  indicator  is  defined  as  the  cost  incurred  to  produce \nanalytical  research  reports,  focussing  on  the  economic \nexploitation  of  children/child  labour,  child  labourers  with \ndisabilities and on the move.    \n\nThe  research  reports  include  those  produced  based  on  a \ndetailed  analysis  of  data  from  the  SIMPOC/CLS  and  MICS \nfocussing  in  children  with  disabilities  and  the  link  to  child \nlabour in Pakistan; an in-depth review of existing interventions \nand  State  responses  to  address  child  labour  in  India;  an \nevaluability assessment, a regional scoping study and the final \nevaluation of the project by ROSA. \n\nThe target for this indicator is at least four (4) and the direct \nbudget allocated is US$ 522,737.  Therefore, the maximum cost \nper research piece will be US$ 130,684.   \n\nThis  indicator  is  defined  as  the  total  population  in  the  30 \ndistricts in the three States of India where the interventions are \nimplemented  and  there  is  a  mechanism  for  voluntary  safe \nreturn.  For the purposes of this indicator, it will only focus on \nthe  population  of  children  in  the  30  districts  of  the  three \nintervention  States.    The  total  child  population  of  these \ndistricts is 172,846,879.   \n\nPopulation  data  for  the  districts  will  be  obtained  from  the \ncensus.   \nThe  target  for  this  indicator  is  172,846,879  children  and  the \ndirect budget allocated is US$ 463,130.  Therefore, the cost per \nchild will be US$ 0.0027. \n\nLevel of \nDisaggregation \n\nthe \nThe  data \nin \nbe \nstudies  will \nby \ndisaggregated \nage, gender and (in \nsome \ninstances) \ndisability.   \n\nMethod of \nComputation \n\nThe  cost  per  research \npiece  is  computed  as \nfollows: \n\n= total funds allocated \nto \nevidence \ngeneration/the  target \nfor this indicator.   \n\nValue \n\nUNICEF  will  directly \nprocure  services  for \nand \nresearch \nall \nthis \nstudies  under \nThe \nprogramme. \nof \nprocurement \nbe \nservices  will \ncompleted through a \ncompetitive  process, \nwhich  will  ensure \nquality  and  effective \npricing. \n\nThe  child  population \nterritories \nin \nthe \nby \ncovered \nmechanisms \nfor \nvoluntary  safe  return \nwill  be \ncalculated \nusing \nGovernment \ndata sources.    \n\nUNICEF  will  support \nthe  development  of \nmechanisms and any \nMoU and/or SOPs.  \n\nThe  data  will  be \ndisaggregated \nby \nage and gender. \n\nThese \ndocuments \nwill support and be in \nline  with \ninter-\ngovernmental \nprocedures.  \n\nSource \n\nwith \n\nContracts \nsub-contractors \nand/or partners \n\nFinal  expenditure \nsheets \n\nFinal \nreports \nelectronic form \n\nresearch \nin \n\nPopulation source \n\nUNICEF reporting \n\nMoU between the \ntransit \nsource, \nand \ndestination \nStates \n\nand/or  \n\nSOPs  manual  on \ncoordination  and \ninformation \nsharing  for  India, \nand \nBangladesh \nNepal  \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 61 OF 62 \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n  \n\x0cPopulation  data \nsource will be the \ncensus.   \n\nUNICEF reporting \n\nThe  SOPs  which \nwill  be  developed \nfunctionalise \nto \nthe \ncase \nmanagement  and \nreferral system.   \n\nUNICEF reporting \n\nEfficiency  \n\nChild \npopulation \nin \ntargeted  areas \ncovered \nby \ncase \nmanagement \nand \nsystem \nPakistan \n\nreferral \nin \n\nChild Population is defined as living people under the age of 18. \nThe population figure is drawn from a referenced source. \n\nThe  targeted  area  is  in  one  district  of  the  Province  of \nBaluchistan.  \n\nCase management and referral mechanism is as per indicator \n2.2 \n\nThe target for this indicator is the child population of Quetta \ndistrict (1,250,000 children) and the direct budget allocated is \nUS$ 227,162.  Therefore the cost per child is US$ 0.18 \n\nThe  child  population \nwill  be \ncalculated \nusing \ncensus  data \nfrom the country. \n\nThe cost per child will \nbe \nas \ncalculated \nfollows: \n\nthe \n\n=  funds  allocated  to \ncase \nsupport \nmanagement \nand \nreferral  system/child \nthe \npopulation \ndistrict.   \n\nin \n\nThe  availability  of  a \ncase  management \nand  referral  system \nthe \nwill \nbenefit \nchild \nentire \npopulation  of \nthe \ndistrict.    It  is  also \nsustainable since the \nprotection \nchild \nsystem  will \nbe \nstrengthened.  \n\nEffectiven\ness  \n\nIncrease in the \nnumber \nof \nchild \nprotection \nstructures  and \nmechanisms \nsupport \nthat \nand  track  case \nmanagement \nof  child  labour \nof \ncases, \nchildren  with \ndisabilities  or \nchildren on the \nmove \n\nChild  protection  structures  and  mechanisms  include,  any \nof/combination of the following: \n\n•  Case management  \n•  Referral mechanisms \n•  Community based child protection mechanisms \n•  Coordination bodies \n\nthat  operate  specifically  to  address  child  labour  including \nchild labourers affected by disability and on the move. \n\nThe  number  of  existing  child  protection  structures  and \nmechanisms in target districts, which address the above issues, \nis 531 in India and 0 in Pakistan. \n\nThe target for this indicator is a total of 3624 child protection \nstructures and mechanisms in India and 167 child  protection \nstructures  and  mechanisms  in  Pakistan.    The  direct  budget \nallocated  is  US$  942,488.    Therefore  the  cost  per  child \nprotection  structure  and/or  mechanism  is  US$  248.60.    The \ncost per child will be calculated at the end of the project period. \n\nThe  effectiveness  of \nthis  intervention  will \nbe  measured  by  the \nnumber  of  children \nreached  through  the \nnew/strengthened \nprotection \nchild \nand \nmechanisms \nstructures \nwhich \naddress  the  needs  of \nlabourers  and \nchild \nchild \nlabourers \naffected by disabilities \nand/or on the move.   \n\nThe \nincreased \navailability  of  child \nprotection structures \nand \nmechanisms \nwhich  support  case \nmanagement of child \nchild \nlabour, \nlabourers affected by \ndisabilities and/or on \nthe move will benefit \nchild \nentire \nthe \npopulation  of \nthe \ntargeted \nStates/Province.   \n\nIt  is  also  sustainable \nchild \nsince \nthe \nprotection \nsystem \nwill be strengthened.  \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 62 OF 62 \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\x0c", 'context_type': 'full_document', 'document_id': 3498, 'document_identity': {'document_id': 3498, 'document_id_source': 'eppi_item_id', 'doi': None, 'first_author': None, 'year': 'None'}, 'parsed_document': {'text': "UNICEF Regional Office for South Asia  \n\nEVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT OF THE UNICEF \n\nCHILD LABOUR: EXPLOITATION OF CHILDREN IN SOUTH ASIA \nPROGRAMME 2019-2023 \n\n© UNICEF/UNI335716 \n\nFINAL REPORT \n\nSubmitted by \nCristina Roccella \nSocial Policy, Child Protection and M&E Consultant \nJuly 2020 \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\x0cContents  \n\nExecutive Summary ......................................................................................................................... 4 \n\n1. Introduction .................................................................................................................................. 6 \n\n1.1 Background and context .................................................................................................... 6 \n\n1.2 Purpose and objectives of the evaluability assessment ................................................... 6 \n\n1.3 Approach ..............................................................................................