.............................................................................................................. 17 \nEfficiency ............................................................................................................................. 17 \nSustainability .......................................................................................................................18 \n\n3.2 Horizontal logic .................................................................................................................18 \nAdequacy .............................................................................................................................18 \nValidity .................................................................................................................................19 \nCompleteness ......................................................................................................................19 \nInformation management ................................................................................................. 20 \nEquity .................................................................................................................................. 20 \n\n3.3 Parameters evaluation matrix .......................................................................................... 21 \n\n4. Recommendations ..................................................................................................................... 22 \n\n4.1 TOC .................................................................................................................................... 22 \n\n4.2 Results framework ........................................................................................................... 23 \n\n4.3 Programme implementation and governance ............................................................... 24 \n\n4.4 Resources allocation ........................................................................................................ 25 \n\n4.5 Detailed comments to the results structure and proposed revisions .......................... 27 \n\n5. Outline for an Evaluation ........................................................................................................... 32 \n\n5.1 Approach ........................................................................................................................... 32 \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 2 OF 62 \n\n \n \n \n\x0c5.2 Methodology .................................................................................................................... 33 \n\n5.3 Recommendation ............................................................................................................. 34 \n\n6. Conclusions ................................................................................................................................ 34 \n\nAnnexes – Evaluability Assessment Tools .................................................................................... 36 \n\nI. Evaluability matrix ............................................................................................................... 37 \n\nII. Semi-structured interviews protocol ................................................................................ 42 \n\nIII. Work Plan – Gantt Chart ................................................................................................... 46 \n\nIV. Reference documents reviewed ...................................................................................... 47 \n\nV. Stakeholders contacted and interviewed ........................................................................ 50 \n\nVI. CLECSAP Indicator definition and monitoring guide ...................................................... 52 \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 3 OF 62 \n\n \n \n \n \n \n\x0cExecutive Summary \n\nUNICEF Regional Office for South Asia, together with UNICEF India and Pakistan Country Offices, is \nimplementing the Child Labour: Exploitation of Children in South Asia Programme (CLECSAP).  The \nproject, supported by DFID and implemented in partnership with governments in South Asia, will last \nfive years. \nThe vision of the programme is that children in South Asia, including  children with disabilities and \nchildren on the move, are better protected from the economic exploitation through effective child \nprotection systems. \nThis programme will contribute to defining the nature of child labour in South Asia, specifically in India \nand Pakistan, and identifying promising practices for further sharing, potential replication and scale up. \nAn  evaluability  assessment  “in  principle”  was  conducted,  with  the  involvement  of  an  external \nconsultant, at the outset of the programme implementation, as a first step of the adaptive learning \napproach that UNICEF intends to promote. \nPrimary and secondary data have been collected and analysed in a fully consultative way, with the \nparticipation  of  many  UNICEF  staff  involved  in  the  implementation  of  the  programme,  and  a \nrepresentative group of counterparts. The assessment responded to four objectives. \nThe first one was to assess the clarity of outcomes, the TOC and the alignment of planned programme \nefforts and strategies to the TOC – as well as to the regional and country context, and UNICEF’s Strategic \nPlan.  The  results’  vertical  logic  has  been  tested  against  eleven  parameters.  Of  these,  five  have \nindicated a full evaluation readiness - relevance; impact; contextualization; efficiency; sustainability; \nwhile  six  would  require  some  adjustments  and  additional  strategic  reflections  to  ensure  the \nprogramme’s full evaluability – clarity, effectiveness, coherence, targeting, equity, risk mitigation. \nThe second objective of the exercise was to assess the adequacy and validity of the indicators, tools \nand  systems  for  monitoring,  measuring  and  verifying  results.  The  horizontal  logic  of  the  results \nframework  was  tested  against  five  parameters,  of  which  one  indicated  a  satisfactory  level  of \nevaluability  (information  management);  three  were  assessed  as  partially  ready  for  evaluation \n(adequacy, validity, completeness) and one was considered not ready for evaluation (equity). \nOverall, no fundamental reasons have been identified to consider the CLECSAP not evaluable: the \nprogramme  planned  results  are  not  unachievable  or  unrealistic,  and  while  risks  and  assumptions \nwould require additional considerations, the overall comprehension of the problem addressed, and \nthe strategic capacity to provide effective responses are visible. \nResource allocations seem to be adequate, in the understanding that results will be achieved with \nthe contribution of the CLECSAP funds, and of other funding sources. \nResponding to the third objective of the evaluability assessment, which was to propose the required \nchanges to make the overall programme more evaluable, recommendations specifically related to \nthe weaknesses identified have been discussed at a final validation meeting with UNICEF teams, and \ncan be grouped under three categories:  \n\nI.  Recommended  adjustments  to  the  TOC,  ensuring the  full  alignment  of  the CLECSAP results \nlogic to the TOC, and linking the expected impact of the programme to the specific problem \naddressed through a clear solution pathway, that includes an increased focus on the strategic \napproaches that add value to the programme; \n\nII.  Recommended amendments to the results framework, which would contribute to the overall \nimprovement of the programme design, and will require punctual interventions involving all \nthe implementing units. These relate to: the increased level of ambition of a few targets; the \ndocumentation  of  strategies  that  contribute  to  the  achievement  of  results  and  related \nindicators; the enhanced quality of means of verification; the refined geographical targeting; \noptions  for  the  definition  of  more  relevant  disaggregation  criteria  for  indicators,  providing \nevidence  of  equity-focused  interventions;  and  the  identification  of  entry  points  for  the \nenhancement of gender equality.  \nIII.  Recommendations  focusing  on  the \n\nimprovement  of  programme \n\nimplementation  and \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 4 OF 62 \n\n \n \n\x0cgovernance, particularly in relation to: the expansion of knowledge management actions, the \nincreased  attention  on  risk  management,  sustained  M&E  mechanisms  and  the  consultation \nwith  children  and  adolescents,  as  right  holders  ultimately  benefitting  from  the  changes \npromoted  by  CLECSAP.  These  recommendations  would  most  likely  require  a  management \nresponse, under the guidance of UNICEF Regional Office.  \n\nThe  fourth  objective  of  the  evaluability  assessment  focused  on  the  outline  of  options  for  the \nprogramme  evaluation design  and  methodology;  among  the  approaches considered,  process  and \ndevelopment evaluations seem to be the most indicated for a complex and composite programme \nlike  CLECSAP.  Contribution  analysis  and  outcome  harvesting  have  been  suggested  as  the  most \neffective methodologies.  \nConsidering that the evaluation design would probably require a mixed-method approach, and the \nidentification  of  the  most  appropriate  timeline  (not  necessarily  coinciding  with  the  end  of  the \nprogramme),  it  has  been  recommended  to  set  up,  during  the  first  phase  of  implementation,  an \nevaluation task force to consult on the mentioned issues, under the leadership of the Regional Office.  \nFinally,  additional  considerations  are  required  to  adapt  the  programme  response  to  the  socio-\neconomic changes caused by the CoViD19 pandemic, also in relation to the likely reallocation of funds \n– in consultation with the donor. \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 5 OF 62 \n\n \n \n \n \n\x0c1. Introduction \n\n1.1 Background and context \n\nUNICEF Regional  Office  for  South  Asia  (ROSA),  together with  UNICEF  India  and  Pakistan  Country \nOffices (COs), is implementing the Child Labour: Exploitation of Children in South Asia Programme \n(CLECSAP).  The project, supported by DFID and implemented in partnership with governments in \nSouth Asia, will last five years (between April 2019 and June 2023). The total budget is GBP 5 million. \nThe vision of the programme is that children in South Asia, including children with disabilities (CWD) \nand children on the move, are better protected from the economic exploitation through effective \nchild protection systems. UNICEF intends to (i) gather evidence on the participation of CWD and child \nlabourers on the move to inform policies and programmes, (ii) strengthen child protection structures \nand mechanisms to prevent and respond to the economic exploitation of children/child labour, and \n(iii) promote community-based mechanisms to reduce the vulnerability of children and protect them \nfrom economic exploitation/child labour.  \nThis programme will contribute to defining the nature of child labour in South Asia, specifically in India \nand Pakistan, and identifying promising practices for further sharing, potential replication and scale up. \nThe programme will be small in scale if compared to the magnitude of the problem, and will be focusing \non  the  structures  and  mechanisms  that  are  currently  in  place  in  three  States  of  India  (Bihar,  Uttar \nPradesh and West Bengal) and one Province of Pakistan (Balochistan) to protect children and identify \nthose  actions  or  additional  actions  which  may  accelerate  the  end  of  this  harmful  practice,  in \ncooperation with sub-national Government counterparts and public service providers. It will also be an \nadaptive learning programme with a structured feedback of the programme learnings directly into the \nprogramme implementation.1 \n\n1.2 Purpose and objectives of the evaluability assessment \n\nIn  keeping  with  UNICEF  guidance  on  conducting  evaluability  assessments,  and  as  detailed  in  the \nCLECSAP proposal, UNICEF is expected to conduct an evaluability assessment (EA) of the programme \nduring the early implementation stage. \nThe purpose of the EA is to assess and strengthen the evaluability of CLECSAP by ensuring the clarity \nof design of the intervention, including the underlying Theory of Change (TOC) and how it has been \ntranslated  into  the  results  framework.  The  EA  provides  an  indication  on  the  degree  to  which  the \ncurrent  indicators  are  valid  and  measurable,  and  on  the  extent  to  which  systems  are  in  place  to \nmeasure  and  monitor  performance  and  achieve  the  results  as  stated  in  the  logical  framework. \nFindings also identify actions that need to be undertaken to strengthen the programme evaluability, \nlogic  and  measurability.  Moreover,  since  CLECSAP  will  use  an  adaptive  learning  approach  to \nprogramming,  conducting  the  EA  at  the  outset  of  the  programme  implementation  can  begin  the \nadaptive process by working to strengthen any weaknesses revealed.2 \nAn independent consultant has been hired to conduct the EA, which has been done remotely for the \nentire duration of the contract.3 \nThe specific objectives of the EA are: \nObjective 1. \nAssess  the  clarity  of  outcomes,  the  TOC  and  the  alignment  of  planned  programme \nefforts  and  strategies  to  the  TOC  –  as  well  as  to  the  regional  and  country  context,  and  UNICEF’s \nStrategic Plan  \n\n1 Cf. UNICEF, Child Labour: Exploitation of Children in South Asia. UNICEF Funding Proposal. DFID \n2  Cf.  UNICEF  Regional  Office  for  South  Asia,  Terms  of  Reference  for  Individual  Consultant,  Assess  the \nEvaluability of the Child Labour: Exploitation of children in South Asia Project 2019-2023, and UNICEF Regional \nOffice for South Asia,  Evaluability Assessment of the UNICEF Child Labour: Exploitation of Children in South \nAsia Programme, 2019-2023. Inception Report, submitted by Cristina Roccella, Consultant, on 16th June 2020.  \n3 The duration of the consultancy is of 35 working days, during the period from 26th May to 1st August 2020 \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 6 OF 62 \n\n \n \n \n\x0cAssess the adequacy and validity of the indicators, tools and systems for monitoring, \n\nObjective 2. \nmeasuring and verifying results \nObjective 3. \nObjective 4. \n\nPropose the required changes to make the overall programme more evaluable \nProvide recommendations on the final evaluation’s design and methodology \n\n1.3 Approach \n\nAccording  to  UNICEF  Guidance  on  EAs,  “evaluability  is  critical  to  strengthening  results-based \nmanagement  and  demonstrating  the  contribution  of  UNICEF-assisted  programmes  to  results  for \nchildren and promoting rights. An EA is a quality assurance tool for good programme design. It is a \ntool  to  help  managers  bring  early  adjustments  and  corrections  to  programme  design  and \nimplementation as needed. Evaluative thinking can and should be applied as early as possible at the \ndesign stage of an intervention.”4 \nThis approach has been applied to the CLECSAP EA, that has been conducted in close collaboration \nwith UNICEF staff and partners in the two COs involved - India and Pakistan - under the supervision \nof UNICEF ROSA Evaluation unit and guidance of UNICEF ROSA Child Protection (CP) unit.  \nThe  assessment  has  been  fully  participatory,  transparent,  and  has  integrated  gender  and  equity \nconsiderations.  The  Human  Rights  Based  Approach  to  programming  has  been  applied  throughout  the \nexercise, paying particular attention to the roles and responsibilities of the duty bearers involved in the \nprogramme, and to the type of response provided to the needs of identified, and particularly vulnerable, \nright holders. \nThe  recommendations  included  in  this  report  focus  on  enhancing  the  overall  evaluability  of  the \nprogramme; they stem from the application of evaluative reasoning to: (i) the analysis of secondary \ndata  gathered  from  the  project  documents  and  other  data  sources;  (ii)  the  consultation  with \nidentified stakeholders through qualitative inquiry tools, which  have been  used to collect primary \ndata  and  to  stimulate  evaluative  thinking  around  the  programme  design,  programming  and \nimplementation.  \n\n1.4 Analytical focus  \n\nConsidering the early implementation stage of the project, the EA is an “evaluability in principle”.5  \nThe  exercise  assessed  the  results’  vertical  logic  –  how  the  programme  design  ensures  verifiable \nconnections  leading  from  activities  to  outputs,  outcomes  and  to  the  programme  impact;  and  the \nhorizontal  logic  –  how  identified  indicators  and  assumptions  allow  to  monitor  and  evaluate  the \nprogramme results.  \nThe evaluability questions have been organised according to a selection of parameters defined by \nUNICEF  guidance,  as  well  as  by  DFID  guidance  and  the  updated  OECD-DAC  evaluation  criteria. \nEvidence derived from the programme documents, as well as from in-depth interviews with various \nstakeholders, has allowed to ‘test’ the programme evaluability against all the identified parameters.6 \n\n1.5 Evaluability assessment methods \n\nThe EA has been carried out in three phases – as indicated in the detailed workplan included in this \ndocument. \n\n4 UNICEF Evaluation Office, Guidance Note for conducting Evaluability Assessments, Draft, October 2018, page 3 \n5 Cf. ibidem \n6 Cf. ibidem; Davies R., An Evaluability Assessment Checklist, in Planning Evaluability Assessments: A Synthesis \nof the Literature with Recommendations, Report of a Study Commissioned by the Department for International \nDevelopment,  January  2015;  OECD,  Better  Criteria  for  Better  Evaluation.  Revised  and  Updated  Evaluation \nCriteria, February 2020. The selected parameters are analyzed in the chapter on Findings of this document, and \nlisted in the Parameters Evaluation Matrix presented in section 3.3  \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 7 OF 62 \n\n \n \n \n \n\x0cThe  inception  phase  has  included  initial  on-line  consultations  with  the  Evaluation  and  CP  units  of \nUNICEF  ROSA  and  with  CP  teams  in  UNICEF  India  and  Pakistan  COs;  the  desk  review  of \nmethodological  guidance  informing  the  proposed  EA  methods  and  tools;  the  initial  review  of \nprogramme documents.7 The final output of this phase is the EA Inception Report. \nFollowing  the  approval  of  the  Inception  Report,  the  assessment  phase  aimed  at  analyzing  the \nprogramme  design  against  defined  research  questions  that  would  allow  to  determine  whether \nconditions are met for a final evaluation.8 Answers were derived from primary and secondary data.  \nPrimary  data  were  collected  through  qualitative  inquiry  tools,  i.e.  one  in-depth,  semi-structured \ninterview9  (on-line)  with  selected  key  informants  involved  in  the  programme  implementation, \nincluding  UNICEF  ROSA,  India  and  Pakistan  CP  teams;  UNICEF  India  and  Pakistan  Monitoring  and \nEvaluation (M&E) teams; Government counterparts and service providers involved in the activities of \nCLECSAP in India (Bihar State) and Pakistan (Balochistan Province).10 \nThe in-depth interviews helped to clarify the stakeholders’ views on the CLECSAP, while stimulating \ntheir  evaluative  thinking  over  the  design,  programming  and  implementation  of  the  project,  and \napplying  adaptive  learning.  The  consultations  with  UNICEF  teams  also  included  a  review  of  the \nvertical  logic  of  the  programme  TOC,  assessing  options  to  further  refine  it  and  ensure  its  full \ncoherence  with  the  programme  logframe.  The  interviews  with  UNICEF  teams  were  followed  by \nwritten questions related to the logical framework, to further collect the teams’ feedback on specific \nfindings and proposed adjustments.  \nIn parallel, the assessment included the in-depth analysis of relevant evidence provided by secondary \ndata sources: global, regional and national documents defining strategic priorities in the areas where \nthe  programme  operates  (relevant  to  the  CLECSAP  contextualization  and  coherence,  targeting, \nequity, and risk mitigation aspects); relevant thematic documents on child labour (including global \nguidance, country-specific situation analyses, evaluations on other child labour projects) providing \nknowledge  informing  the  assessment  of  the  expected  relevance,  effectiveness  and  impact  of  the \nprogramme;  the  programme  documents,  relevant  to  the  assessment  of  all  the  evaluability \nparameters, and in particular of the clarity, efficiency, sustainability aspects. These documents have \nalso  been  the  main  source  of  data  for  the  full  assessment  of  the  tools  available  for  monitoring, \nmeasuring and verifying results, focusing in particular on the adequacy, validity, completeness and \nequity of indicators, and on the information management system in place to ensure the regular and \nrelevant monitoring of the programme. \nThe final output of the second phase was the submission to UNICEF ROSA of the draft EA Report and \nrelated Power Point (PPT) presentation, including key findings, recommendations and conclusions. \nThe third, validation phase included a virtual meeting with UNICEF Regional and CO staff to validate \nthe key findings of the draft EA report, providing the opportunity to adjust factual or interpretation \nerrors and further reflect in a consultative way on the recommendations. This document has been \nfinalized in conclusion of the third phase. \n\n1.6 Limitations of the Evaluability Assessment \n\nThe principal limitation of the EA is related to the situation determined by the CoVid19 Pandemic, due \nto  which  the  full  exercise  was  conducted  remotely.  This  has  posed  some  constraints  to  the \nconsultant’s capacity to map data points and stakeholders, and select the interlocutors based on a \ndirect  assessment.  The  constraints  related  to  the  ongoing  lock-down,  the  limited  amount  of  time \n\n7 The full list of all the documents analyzed throughout the EA is included as Annex IV to this document \n8 These are listed in the Evaluability matrix included in Annex I to this document \n9 The interviews protocol is included as Annex II to this document  \n10 The selected province of Pakistan is the one where the entire CLECSAP will be implemented; in India, three \nStates are involved in the programme (Bihar, Uttar Pradesh and West Bengal); it was decided to sample one \nState  for  in-depth  interviews,  taking  into  consideration  the  degree  of  engagement  of  counterparts  in  the \nimplementation of the CLECSAP, their evaluative thinking competency, and the availability for the interview at \na time of major constraints related to the CoViD19 pandemic. The State selected by the India CP team is Bihar. \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 8 OF 62 \n\n \n \n \n\x0cmade  available  by  the  respondents,  and  unstable  connectivity  conditions,  have  reduced  the \ninteraction with Government counterparts and service providers to a minimum. \nA secondary limitation is related to the fact that, considering the very initial implementation stage of \nthe CLECSAP, it was not possible to conduct an evaluability “in practice”. Therefore, the parameters \nincluded  in  the  evaluability  matrix  were  tested  against  the  design  and  inception  phase  of  the \nprogramme, and it was not possible to assess implementation and management arrangements. For \nthe same reason, the possibility to assess the adequacy of budget allocations – and derive an analysis \non the programme efficiency - was quite limited. \n\n2. The CLECSAP TOC, results chain and strategic approaches \n\nThe CLECSAP and its results logic are described in the set of documents presented to the donor and \ndefined during the inception phase of the programme, including: \n\n▪  The programme document, including a TOC and outcome statements, activities and mode of \n\ndelivery - submitted to DFID at the beginning of 2019; \n\n▪  The programme budget, finalized a few months after the submission of the proposal; \n▪  A  Logical  Framework,  including  outcomes,  outputs,  indicators,  indicative  targets,  and \nactivities,  submitted  in  2019  and  updated  during  the  inception  phase  of  the  programme \n(January 2020); \n\n▪  An Indicator Definition and Monitoring Guide, including all the results and related indicators, \nwith  detailed  baselines,  targets,  definitions,  method  of  computation,  sources/means  of \nverification (MoVs), levels of disaggregation, and additional information, developed during \nthe inception phase (February-March 2020); \n\n▪  Action Plans defined during the inception phase for the two COs involved, and for the RO; \n▪  A Timeline revised during the inception phase; \n▪  A second version of the TOC, revised in consultation with the COs at the programme inception \nmeeting (February 2020), and circulated among a range of stakeholders for their comments.  \nStarting  from  the  analysis  of  the  situation  of  children  involved  in  child  labour  in  South  Asia,  the \nCLECSAP proposes a strategic approach complementing the programmes that have been ongoing \nfor decades to end child labour in the region and focusing on child protection. The intent of CLECSAP \nis to add value to the existing programmes and learn from them, identifying good practices that bring \nresults  for  children  and  can  be  shared  with  other  countries  in  the  region,  as  well  as  potentially \nadapted for scale up. The programme applies an adaptive approach in ensuring that its delivery is \ncontinuously informed by the learning generated throughout the duration of the programme. \nThe vision of the programme is that children in South Asia, including CWD and children on the move, \nare better protected from economic exploitation through effective child protection systems. Within \nthis  vision,  the  programme  is  structured  around  three  outcomes,  focusing  on  evidence  to  inform \npolicies, system strengthening and community mobilization: \n\n▪ \n\n▪ \n\n▪ \n\nOUTCOME 1: Government and partners within and across countries support the generation and \nuse  of  robust  data  and  evidence  to  inform  policy  and  programming,  track  progress,  and \ndocument lessons on the economic exploitation of children/child labour, including CWD and \nchildren on the move. \n\nOUTCOME 2: Effective child protection structures and mechanisms are in place to prevent and \nrespond to the economic exploitation of children/child labour, including CWD and children on \nthe move. \n\nOUTCOME 3: Community-based mechanisms are operational to prevent and respond to the \neconomic exploitation of children/child labour, including CWD and children on the move.11 \n\n11 UNICEF, Child Labour: op. cit., page 3  \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 9 OF 62 \n\n \n \n \n\x0cFor each outcome, specific outputs have been defined that relate to the contribution of the three \noffices involved in the implementation of the programme (UNICEF ROSA, UNICEF India and UNICEF \nPakistan COs). Each outcome and output has specific indicators, baselines and targets, definitions, \nmethod of computation, MoVs, and levels of disaggregation, described in the Indicator Definition and \nMonitoring  Guide,  which  defines  also  which  implementing  unit  is  responsible  for  delivering  and \nreporting against them.  \nAction  plans  have  been  prepared  by  the  two  COs  involved,  linking  activities  and  sub-activities  to \nspecific outputs and outcomes. \nThe programme results are also included in the programme  TOC, that has been revised through a \nconsultation with partners and counterparts during the inception phase of the programme (March \n2020). Starting from a problem statement focusing on the persistence of child labour in the region, \nthe TOC lists thirteen underlying drivers and causes, and six bottlenecks that look at the weaknesses \nof  the  system  called  to  respond  to  child  labour,  from  a  child  protection  perspective.  The  four \nstrategies included in the TOC respond directly to the bottlenecks identified, and are linked to five \noutcome statements, involving Government counterparts, communities and children.  \nThe impact result makes reference to boys and girls growing up in an environment with systems and \nstructures which prevent and respond to child labour. While the outcomes are  defined as project \nresults (i.e. are directly attributable to the CLECSAP funds and management) the impact statement \ncan be considered a programme result, as it relates to institutional change, quality and coverage of \nservices, and behavioral change. No strategic results – directly producing a change in the enjoyment \nof rights of children and women – are included in the CLECSAP TOC.12 \nA set of assumptions is included in the programme document; a revised set, partially including the \ninitial ones, is incorporated in the updated TOC. A paragraph on risk assessment, where three risks \nare identified, can be found in the programme document. \nThe strategies promoted within CLECSAP – support for national and community ownership; UNICEF \nsystem-based  approach;  capacity  development;  policy  advocacy;  partnerships;  knowledge \nmanagement;  service  delivery;  South-South  cooperation;  adaptive  approach  to  programme  -  are \ndescribed  under  the  Governance,  Adaptation  and  Coordination  chapter  of  the  programme \ndocument. \n\n3. Findings  \n\nThe  findings  of  this  EA,  formulated  combining  the  consultant’s  direct  observation  of  primary  and \nsecondary  data,  the  opinions  and  feedback  of  the  stakeholders  consulted,  and  considerations \nderived from methodological guidance, are reported against all the parameters considered for the \nassessment, and organized according to the specific objectives of the EA.  \n\n3.1 Vertical logic \n\nThe first objective of the EA is to assess the clarity of outcomes, the TOC and the alignment of planned \nprogramme  efforts  and  strategies  to  the  TOC  –  as  well  as  to  the  regional  and  country  context,  and \nUNICEF’s Strategic Plan.  \nIt  mainly  relates  to  the  vertical  logic  that  links  activities  to  outputs,  and  outputs  to  outcomes,  in \nachieving  a  change  at  impact  level,  starting  from a  specific  problem  statement.  It  has  to  do  with \nvarious aspects of the programme design and governance, which have been taken into consideration \nwhile  analyzing  the  programme  documents  and  stimulating  evaluative  thinking  among  the \nstakeholders involved. The analysis focused on verifying if enough and relevant evidence is provided \nin the programme design to demonstrate that all the evaluability criteria have been sufficiently taken \n\n12  For  the  categorization  of  results  cf.  UNICEF  Evaluation  Office  and  Division  of  Policy  and  Planning, \nUnderstanding  Results  Based  Programme  Planning  and  Management.  Tools  to  Reinforce  Good  Programming \nPractice, September 2003, page 14  \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 10 OF 62 \n\n \n \n \n\x0cinto consideration, providing the conditions required for the programme to be considered evaluable.  \nThe analytical findings related to each parameter are described below. A summary grading is then \nreported  in  the  Parameters  Evaluation  Matrix  at  the  end  of  this  chapter.  For  easy  reference,  the \nrevised TOC is included here below. \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 11 OF 62 \n\n \n \n\x0cClarity \n\nWhile  the  CLECSAP  logframe  follows  a  quite  clear  vertical  logic,  and  indicators  are  adequately \ndescribed,13 the results logic is not fully reflected in the TOC. The TOC starts from a broad problem \nstatement on the persistence and the prevalence of child labour, and ends with an impact statement \nnot directly linked to the problem statement through a clear solution pathway.14 While the drivers \nand  causes  listed  in  the  TOC  relate  to  the  problem  statement  as  it  is  formulated,  the  included \nstrategies and outcomes don’t seem to indicate interventions that are sufficient to provide a solution \nto  the  identified  problem.  The  TOC doesn’t  identify  a  specific  segment  of  the  causal chain  that  is \naddressed by the CLECSAP.  \nIn  fact,  the  programme  document  specifies  that  the  problem  addressed  by  the  CLECSAP  is  not \ndirectly child labour, but rather the capacity of the system of services, and of communities, to identify \nand respond to child labour; the underlying causes of the systemic weaknesses are described in the \nTOC as ‘bottlenecks’, added one layer above the drivers and causes, which indicate in a clearer way \nthe problem addressed and the type of solutions required.  \nThe  following  layer  of  the  TOC’s  vertical  structure  includes  interventions  that  are  defined  as \n“strategies”, but rather indicate some of the actions planned to achieve the expected outcomes; i.e., \nthey correspond to the programme outputs (although they are not formulated literally as the outputs \nincluded in the logframe).  \nThe programme strategies, although briefly described in the programme document,15 are not made \nexplicit  in  the  TOC.  Considering  their  relevance  to  the  programme  effectiveness,  as  repeatedly \nunderlined by various stakeholders interviewed,16 maintaining strategic approaches implicitly related \nto the programme, but not explicitly located (and documented) is a constraint to the full evaluability \nof the CLECSAP – in particular as far as the system-based approach, capacity development, policy \nadvocacy, knowledge management (KM) and the adaptive approach to programme are concerned. \nWithout these, it would be quite difficult to perceive and evaluate the connections that would make \nof the various interventions promoted by the CLECSAP the tiles of a coherent and comprehensive \npicture. \nFurthermore, the outcomes included in the TOC don’t fully correspond to the ones included in the \nLogframe – they are five instead of three. \nThe impact level result, acknowledging the specific approach and results chain of the programme, \nrefers to a systemic change (“an environment with effective systems and structures which prevent \nand respond to child labour”) as the context in which boys and girls grow up.  \nAssumptions  are  added  to  the  TOC,  partially  addressing  specific  conditions  required  for  the \nachievement of the programme outcomes and impact, and partially referring to the broader issue of \nchild labour. These ones are however too broad and generic,17 and don’t include sufficiently specific \nresponses to the drivers and causes that are not addressed by the CLECSAP. \nA few adjustments to the TOC can be proposed that would enhance its logic from the perspective of \n\n13 Although with a few adjustments required, as described in the second part of this chapter  \n14 “The solution pathway analysis involves translating the  cause-and effect analysis of the situation and the \ncontext assessment into results – outcomes and impacts. The best points of action along the solution path are \nselected in line with where the programme’s impacts can be placed, as well as where to develop outputs and \nactivities.”  Cf.  United  Nations  Development  Group,  Theory  of  Change.  Concept  Note,  UNDG  LAC  Secretariat, \nOctober 2016, page 3  \n15 UNICEF, Child Labour: op. cit., pages 23-24 \n16 And as highlighted also in relation to the horizontal logic of the programme results. See section 3.2 of this \ndocument \n17  E.g.:  “Economic  growth  will  contribute  to  reducing  child  labour”;  “The  interventions  of  other  partners \nsupport efforts to end child labour and improve legislation”. \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 12 OF 62 \n\n \n \n \n \n \n\x0cresults-based programme planning,18 allowing to report systematically on all the components of the \nprogramme, and thus enhancing its evaluability.19 \n\nRelevance \n\nThe programme document makes it explicit that the target groups and direct beneficiaries of the \nprogramme are – applying a human-rights based approach – those who would be considered “duty \nbearers” towards children victim of economic exploitation: “UNICEF will work with governments and \ncommunities. The government will be at regional (SAIEVAC) national (relevant ministries in India and \nPakistan), and subnational level (in select areas of India and Pakistan). The intention is to strengthen \nthe capacity of these civil and public servants and community leaders, the social service workforce, \nwho in turn assist children involved in or at risk of economic exploitation/child labour.”20 \nThe  enhancement  of  duty  bearers’  awareness  of  the  specific  needs  of  identified  categories  of \nchildren involved in child labour, and of successful models of interventions; of their capacity to deliver \nservices in an integrated manner; and of their understanding of risks and consequences of children’s \neconomic  exploitation,  as  well  as  of  their  capacity  to  identify  and  respond  to  children  in  need  of \nprotection, will be decisive to achieve the change defined at impact level, i.e. the strengthening of \nthe system’s capacity to identify and protect children involved in child labour. \nIn  this sense,  the  programme  objectives  are clearly  relevant  to  the needs  of  the  identified  target \ngroups, and promote strategic approaches explicitly mentioned in specific guidance documents.21  \n\nEffectiveness \n\nThe programme design puts great emphasis on the systemic approach promoted by UNICEF in child \nprotection, which would ensure the integration of interventions aimed at reducing child labour. In so \ndoing,  the  programme  aims  at  achieving  what  has  been  defined  by  one  of  the  Government \ncounterparts  interviewed  as  the  ‘most  relevant  feature  of  the  CLECSAP’,  i.e.  the  convergence  of \ninterventions aimed at responding to child labour.22 \nWhile an integrated social services system clearly promotes the inter-sectoral cooperation of all the \nservice  providers  called  to  respond  to  the  protection  needs  of  children  and  families,  the \nstrengthening of inter-sectoral cooperation is less explicit in  the programme design – beyond the \nreference  to \nin  the  description  of  the  packages  of \ninterventions  promoted  under  outcome  3.  This  strategic  approach  is  reportedly  already  pursued \nacross UNICEF programmes,23 and should be further expanded and documented to avoid that the \naggregate impact of the interventions put in place will be less than the sum of their parts.24 This would \n\ninter-departmental  cooperation  made \n\n18 UNICEF guidance is particularly helpful in this sense: “Results Based Programme Planning ensures that the \nsum of interventions is sufficient to achieve the expected result. Interventions must not only be necessary, but \nalso sufficient to achieve the expected result. If a problem is caused by three conditions, addressing only one \nor two conditions will not ensure that the problem will be solved. If the UNICEF-assisted programme can only \naddress one of the three necessary conditions, other Government departments or partner agencies need to \ncommit  themselves  to  deal  with  the  remaining  causes.  We  are  assuming  that  the  others  will  do  what  is \nnecessary and will record this as a critical planning assumption”. UNICEF Evaluation Office and Division of Policy \nand Planning, op. cit., page 2 \n19 The suggested revision of the TOC is visualized in a slide of the Power Point presentation prepared for the EA \nvalidation meeting \n20 UNICEF, Child Labour: op. cit., page 25 \n21  Reference  is  made  in  particular  to:  Global  Social  Service  Workforce  Alliance  &  UNICEF,  Guidelines  to \nStrengthen the Social Service Workforce for Child Protection, February 2019; ILO & UNICEF, Covid-19 and Child \nLabour: a Time of Crisis, a Time to Act, 2020  \n22 Reference is made to the in-depth interview to Mr. Rohit Raj Singh and Ms. Namita Shekhar, Bihar State, India \n(10th July 2020; recording available) \n23 As testified by both the CP teams operating in India and Pakistan (in-depth interviews of the 25th June and 1st \nJuly 2020; recording available) \n24 For a similar reflection cf. Quattri M. & Watkins K., Child Labour and Education. A Survey of Slum Settlements \nin Dhaka, ODI, December 2016, page 9 \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 13 OF 62 \n\n \n \n \n\x0cbe possible, in terms of KM and policy advocacy, through the actions related to outcome 1; and in \nterms of service delivery, throughout the work related to outcome 2. \n\nImpact \n\nThe  programme  document  is  quite  explicit  in  describing  the  expected  impact  of  the  programme, \nwhich will have to be evaluated as the increased capacity of the system of services to identify and \nrespond to cases of child labour, with particular attention to children with disabilities and children on \nthe move. While the impact statement is not pitched at the same level of the problem statement, it \nclearly represents the end result of a solution pathway addressing systemic weaknesses. \nThe evidence generation and KM components of the programme show a great potential in triggering \nmultiple reflections and actions at national and international level, through the sharing of new data, \nmodels of interventions and good practices, and building on  new knowledge for upstream, policy \nadvocacy work at inter-sectoral level. This type of impact can be evaluated through the analysis of \nhow  specific  strategies  have  been  promoted  throughout  the  programme  implementation.  Some \nactions related to this component might need to be further highlighted and refined, to provide for a \nfull evaluability of the obtained results. \nApplying  plausible  association  reasoning,  it  would  be  possible  to  understand  how  the  changes \nattributable  to  the  CLECSAP  will  in  the  longer-term  affect  child  labour  –  by  providing  effective \nresponses to the need of identified children, and contributing to reducing the phenomenon. \nThe level at which the impact of the programme is pitched will also determine the type of evaluation \nrequired. \n\nCoherence \n\nUnderstandably,  the  CLECSAP  design  is  coherent  with  the  approach  promoted  in  DFID  Business \nCase25  –  although  the  articulation  of  interventions  defined  by  UNICEF  during  the  programme \nnegotiation phase seems to have lost the narrower and more coherent focus exclusively on children \nwith  disabilities  and  labour,  and  on  the  gender  dimensions  of  this  specific  phenomenon,  initially \ndescribed in DFID’s document. \nAddressing child labour certainly responds to regional and national priorities, highlighted in several \ndocuments – in particular the Regional SAIEVAC plan for the prevention and elimination of all forms \nof  child  labour,  Country  Programme  Documents  (CPD)  and  Committee  on  the  Rights  of  the  Child \n(CRC) Concluding Observations for both India and Pakistan, and the UNDAF for Pakistan.26 The CRC \nConcluding  Observations  for  Pakistan  make  also  a  clear  reference  to  the  need  to  focus  on  the \nsituation  of  CWD,  promoting  –  among  other  recommendations  –  improved  data  collection.  The \nactions included in the CLECSAP contribute at least to one result of UNICEF global Strategic Plan,27 \nand clearly apply the first of the eight change strategies included in the document.28 Last but not \nleast, the very recent joint publication of UNICEF and ILO on the implications of the CoViD19 pandemic \n\n25 DFID, Asia Regional Child Labour Programme. Business Case, May 2018 \n26 Cf. SAIEVAC, Regional Action Plan for the Prevention and Elimination of all Forms of Child Labour in South \nAsia 2016-2021, SAARC SAIEVAC Regional Secretariat; UNICEF Executive Board, Country Programme Document. \nPakistan, July 2017; United Nations, Pakistan One United Nations Programme III (OP III) 2018-2022; Committee \non the Rights of the Child, Concluding observations on the fifth periodic report of Pakistan, United Nations, July \n2016; UNICEF Executive Board, Country Programme Document. India, July 2017; Committee on the Rights of \nthe Child, Concluding observations on the combined third and fourth periodic reports of India, United Nations, \nJuly  2014.  Surprisingly  enough,  the  Sustainable  Development  Framework  for  India  doesn’t  make  explicit \nreference  to  child  labour  as  a  priority  area  of  intervention  (although  a  national  child  labour  project  is \nmentioned). \n27 “Output statement 3.a: Countries have strengthened child protection systems for prevention and response \nservices to address violence against children. Output indicator 3.a.2 . Number of countries with legislative and \npolicy framework to eliminate the worst forms of child labour.” United Nations Children’s Fund,  Final results \nframework of the UNICEF Strategic Plan, 2018-2021, E/ICEF/2017/18, September 2017, pages 27-28  \n28 Cf. UNICEF, Strategic Plan 2018-2021. Executive Summary, UNICEF, 2018, page 26 \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 14 OF 62 \n\n \n \n \n \n\x0con child labour calls for an immediate response which resonates with the CLECSAP interventions (in \nparticular related to outcome 2).29 \nHowever, almost all the mentioned documents, as well as the most relevant guidance documents \npromoting  strategies  to  accelerate  the  eradication  of  child  labour,  don’t  make  reference  to  the \nstrategic approach proposed by the CLECSAP as a key intervention. \nWhile the production of evidence for KM and advocacy purposes is always recommended, and while \nreferences can be found to community-based interventions aimed at reducing negative social norms \nand  attitudes,  the  two  areas  of  intervention  constantly  considered  fundamental  to  address  child \nlabour are social protection and education;30 although with various caveats, the financial constraints \nof households are widely recognized as the primary cause of child labour,31 as well as the  relative \nimportance attributed by families to education, and the consequent low investment on that.32  \nWhile many among these documents recommend an integrated approach to child labour, there seem \nnot to be sufficient acknowledgment of the fact that the integration of interventions can happen \nthrough the establishment of a systemic model of child protection services, realized through case \nmanagement  –  which  is  indeed  the  approach  proposed  by  the  CLECSAP.  As  underlined  by  one \nGovernment  representative  interviewed,  through  case  management  social  workers  function  as  a \n‘bridge’ to enable families and children to access schemes and services that would respond to their \nneeds, and reduce negative coping strategies, such as the involvement in child labour.33  \nIn this sense, the programme can represent an innovative way of enhancing models of integrated \nresponse to child labour, not only through the strengthening of service provision,34 but also through \n\n29 Cf. ILO & UNICEF, Covid-19 and Child Labour: a Time of Crisis, a Time to Act, 2020 \n30 Of the documents previously mentioned, only the two CPDs for Pakistan and India make direct reference to \nthe strengthening of case management practices (including referral mechanisms) as a systemic approach to \nensuring children’s protection from any form of violence and exploitation. The publication on CoVid19 and child \nlabour  makes  a  very  clear  reference  to  the  essential  role  of  qualified  social  service  workers.  The  other \ndocuments  promote  the  expansion  of  social  protection  schemes  to  reduce  the  economic  vulnerability  of \nfamilies, and improved access to education as a deterrent to the full involvement of children in child labour. \nThis is also true for global publications promoting strategic approaches to fight against child labour, including: \nSAIEVAC, Regional Action Plan for the Prevention and Elimination of all Forms of Child Labour in South Asia 2016-\n2021,  SAARC  SAIEVAC  Regional  Secretariat;  UNICEF,  Child Labor and  UNICEF in Action: Children at the Centre, \nUNICEF,  May  2014;  ILO,  Ending  child  labour  by  2025:  A  Review  of  Policies  and  Programmes;  OPM,  2016  India: \nSummative  Evaluation  of  UNICEF  India’s  Cotton  Corridors  Project:  Preventing  Exploitation  and  Protecting \nChildren’s Rights in Andhra Pradesh and Karnataka, in UNICEF Evaluation Database (extract); FUNDAMENTALS \n& ILO Regional Office for Africa, Meta-Analysis of Evaluations on ILO Child Labour Programmes and Projects in \nAfrica 2009-2014, ILO, 2017; Samantroy E. et al., State of Child Workers in India. Mapping Trends, V.V. Giri National \nLabour  Institute  &  UNICEF;  ILO,  Evaluation:  Preventing  and  Eliminating  Child  Labour  in  identified  hazardous \nSectors  in  India  (Child  Labour  Component)  –  INDUS,  ILO  Evaluation  Summaries.  DFID’s  Business  Case  on  Asia \nRegional Child Labour Programme makes reference to the Agency’s conceptual framework on child labour that \n“identifies  the  multiple  causes  of  child  labour,  including  household  poverty,  access  to  education  and  social \nnorms, as well as what it terms a permissive environment including weak legislation and law enforcement and \nunethical business models”(op. cit., page 9).   \n31  Cf.  for  example  the  recommendations  of  OPM  to  UNICEF  on  the  cotton  corridors  project:  “it  becomes \nimperative  that  a  child  protection  strategy  (targeted  at  eliminating  child  labour)  acknowledges  and \nincorporates the angle of financial constraints on households that might disable from adopting favourable child \nprotection behaviours”. OPM, op. cit. \n32 As confirmed also by the Government counterparts interviewed during the EA. Reference is made to the in-\ndepth interview to Mr. Rohit Raj Singh and Ms. Namita Shekhar, Bihar State, India (10th July 2020; recording \navailable);  and  to  the  interview  to  Mr.  Nasir  Baloch,  Balochistan  Province,  Pakistan  (17th  July,  recording \nunavailable) \n33 Reference is made to the in-depth interview to Mr. Nasir Baloch, mentioned in the previous footnote \n34  Coherently  with  the  guidelines  on  strengthening  social  workforce  for  child  protection.  Cf.  Global  Social \nService Workforce Alliance & UNICEF, op. cit. \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 15 OF 62 \n\n \n \n \n \n\x0cthe production of evidence that can be used to further enhance the effectiveness of policies and \nservices, and through community mobilization.  \nSeveral stakeholders interviewed for the EA made reference to combating child labour as a national \npriority, once again focusing mainly on social protection schemes, and on education programmes. \nLikewise, several UNICEF CP staff involved in the implementation of the programme mentioned their \nongoing efforts to coordinate with education and social protection colleagues, in order to ensure a \ncomprehensive and effective response to child labour. \nTo ensure the full coherence of the programme design, efforts should be made to make inter-sectoral \ncooperation around the project more explicit in the results framework, or to include this among the \nstrategies  promoted  by  the  programme  and  highlighted  in  the  TOC;  this  would  ensure  the \ndocumentation of this work to allow for a full evaluability.  \n\nTargeting \n\nThe  programme  design  clearly  identifies  governments  and  communities  as  primary  beneficiaries. \nSpecific categories of children at risk of economic exploitation (including CWD and children on the \nmove)  can  be  identified  as  intended  indirect  beneficiaries;  this  can  be  done  applying  plausible \nassociation reasoning, since there are no indicators referring to the direct outreach of children in the \nprogramme-supported initiatives.35 Only two indicators refer directly to beneficiaries, in both cases \nduty bearers (service providers and community members). \nAlthough the programme document, outputs and indicators  clearly define the geographical areas \nwhere  activities  will  be  implemented,  the  outcomes  are  presented  without  specification  of  the \ngeographic targeting. The explicit indication of targeted areas (and possibly of timeframes) would \nmake the programme results easier to evaluate. \n\nEquity \n\nThe entire vision of the CLECSAP aims at identifying, protecting and understanding the specific needs \nof particularly vulnerable and marginalized categories of children, i.e. children victims of economic \nexploitation, and among them CWD and children on the move. Therefore, it is evident that equity \nconsiderations are at the core of the entire programme design. Specific attention is paid to the rights \nof the indirect beneficiaries of the programme, and the engagement of duty bearers in providing a \nmore solid, durable and effective response to their needs highlights the application of the human \nrights-based approach to programming promoted by UNICEF. \nWhile  outcome  and  output  statements  clearly  reflect  equity  considerations  to  programming,  the \nsame cannot be said for the related indicators, which are mostly referring to intellectual outputs and \ntherefore  provide  little  space  for  disaggregation  (by  age,  gender  and  disabilities  only  for  two \nindicators). As a consequence, very limited evidence will be provided allowing for an evaluation of \nthe equity focus of the programme.36 \nAlthough gender considerations are included in the narrative programme description – and reference \nis made to gender specific risk mitigations integrated in empowerment programmes and community \ninterventions supported by the CLECSAP37 - there is no specific reference to the gender dimension \neither in the results statements or in the formulation of indicators. In particular, there is no evidence \nof the gender analysis that according to DFID’s Business Case was to be integrated in the research \nmethodology on child labour and disability,38 or of other actions and strategies focused at enhancing \na gender transformative approach.  \n\n35 Cf. UNICEF, Child labour: op. cit., pages 25-26 \n36 A more detailed analysis is reported in the paragraph on equity of section 3.2  \n37 Cf. UNICEF, Child labour: op. cit., page 26 \n38  “The  UNICEF  component  on  child  labour  and  disability  will  integrate  gender  analysis  into  the  research \nmethodology  and  will  design  interventions  that  address  the  links  between  gender  and  disability  in  the \nexploitation of vulnerable children”. DFID, op. cit., page 11 \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 16 OF 62 \n\n \n \n \n \n\x0cRisk mitigation \n\nAs previously mentioned, a set of assumptions are included in the CLECSAP programme document, \npertaining both to programme governance and programme implementation; these refer mostly to \noutcome  1,  and  at  least  in  one  case  are  more  related  to  issues  that  fall  under  the  control  of  the \nprogramme (and therefore cannot be considered assumptions) than under the control of others.39 \nA revised set of assumptions has been included in the second version of the TOC, taking into partial \nconsideration the ones included in the programme document. The revised assumptions relate both \nto the programme implementation as it is defined by the results chain, and to the wider set of causes \nunderlying the persistence of child labour. These ones are however very generic, and it wouldn’t be \npossible  to  establish  a  link  between  the  variation  of  these  assumptions  and  the  achievement  of \nresults related to the CLECSAP. \nAs far as the risk assessment is concerned, a few risks are mentioned in a dedicated chapter of the \nprogramme document, but they are not fully assessed and rated, and therefore a specific type of \nresponse is not defined. Considering the complex and composite outline of the programme, more \ndetailed  risk  matrixes  -  identifying  risks  for  each  operating  environment,  rating  them  in  terms  of \nlikelihood and impact, and deciding the type of response required40 - would not only comply with \nUNICEF’s  enterprise  risk management  principles,  but  also  allow  to  justify  programmatic decisions \nthat have been made in the formulation of results and the definition of indicators.41 \n\nContextualization \n\nAlthough  the  programme  document  doesn’t  provide  evidence  on  the  distribution  of  roles  and \nresponsibilities  between  UNICEF  and  counterparts,  and  despite  the  multiple  and  complex \ninteractions required by the programme – both at policy and at service delivery levels – there is no \nevidence  of  any  particular  concern  of  the  stakeholders  interviewed  on  the  interaction  of  all  the \npartners  involved,  and  the  understanding  of  their  respective  tasks.  As  mentioned  by  one  of  the \nrespondents  of  the  India  CO  team,42  the  interaction  and  cooperation  of  various  service  providers \ncalled to respond to the needs of children involved in labour is a challenge inherent to the promotion \nof a systemic approach to child protection. Specific concerns, if any, should be included in the TOC \nassumptions, or in the assessed risks. \n\nEfficiency \n\nConsidering the very early stage of implementation of the programme, resource allocations could be \nassessed only through the analysis of provisional budgets. Resources seem to be aligned with the \nresults,  and  in  most  instances  sufficient  to  achieve  the  planned  outputs  and  outcomes.  The \nprogramme  governance  ensured  by  UNICEF,  and  described  in  the  programme  document,  also  in \nconsideration  of  the  Agency’s  operational  standards,  seem  to  provide  sufficient  evidence  of  solid \nmechanisms in place to ensure the programme’s efficiency.  \nProgramme efficiency is also referred to in two value-for-money indicators included in the Indicator \nDefinition and Monitoring Guide; in these instances, the budget allocated to specific outputs is divided \nby the entire child population of the region targeted by the action, thus determining the cost per \nchild of the programme investment. Although indicators have been defined underlining the right to \n\n39 “Evidence generated identifies effective actions to reduce the economic exploitation of children”. UNICEF, \nChild labour: op. cit., page 35 \n40  Such  as  avoidance,  acceptance,  transfer/share  or  control.  Cf.  UNICEF  Enterprise  Risk  Management, \nInstruction 1: Risk Assessment and Reporting, 2015, page 5 \n41 For example, India CO has justified the absence of indicators related to the number of beneficiaries reached \nthough service delivery based on the evaluation of the risk of not having access to public administrative data. \nSuch  risk  should  be  included  in  a  matrix,  and  depending  on  the  evaluation  of  its  likelihood  and  impact,  the \ndecision  to  avoid  the  risk  (and  therefore  exclude  such  types  of  indicators)  or  take  the  risk  (and  include \nindicators on beneficiaries) can be justified  \n42 Cf. in-depth interview with UNICEF Child Protection team in India, 25th June 2020 (recording available)  \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 17 OF 62 \n\n \n \n \n \n\x0caccess to services of the entire child population, and therefore the potential utilization of services by \nevery child in the area of reference,43 it would be advisable to reconsider the indicator’s denominator \nas the estimated percentage of the child population at risk of being involved in migration and child \nlabour, or with protection needs. \nThe budget allocated to Pakistan CO is sensibly lower than the one of the other implementing units, \nand this is reportedly justified by the fact that the CO is utilizing other resources to contribute to the \nachievement of results. This seems to be the result of an actively planned and managed convergence \nof  resources;  in  these  cases,  however,  reported  results  will  not  be  entirely  attributable  to  DFID \nsupport to CLECSAP.  \nA  few  adjustments  to  the  programme  proposed  in  Chapter  4  could  require,  if  accepted,  a  slight \nincrease  in  budget  allocations,  which  would  need  to  be  negotiated  with  the  donor  in  the \nunderstanding that such increase would provide for an improved evaluability of the programme. \n\nSustainability \n\nSince  the  programme  doesn’t  directly  implement  service  delivery,  but  aims  at  sustaining  and \nstrengthening the systems of services already in place to respond to the protection needs of children, \nsustainability can be considered one of the most prominent features of the CLECSAP, fully integrated \nin the programme strategic approach. Likewise, the production of evidence widely shared to inform \nservice  modelling  and  replication  of  good  practices,  will  allow  to  extend  the  impact  of  the \nprogramme beyond the implementation timeline, and potentially multiply the positive effect of the \nresults achieved through continuous KM actions.. \n\n3.2 Horizontal logic \n\nThe second objective of the EA is to  assess the adequacy and validity of the indicators, tools and \nsystems for monitoring, measuring and verifying results.  \nThis component of the assessment relates to the horizontal logic that links outputs and outcomes to \nthe identified indicators, verifying whether the conditions are set to provide sufficient and relevant \nevidence for the evaluation of the programme.  \nAs in the previous section, a set of parameters have been taken into consideration while analyzing \nprimary and secondary data, and stimulating a reflection with the UNICEF professionals interviewed. \nThe analysis focused on verifying if each of the parameters was sufficiently considered and reflected \nin the indicators’ design, contributing to the evaluability of the programme. The findings for each \nparameter  are  described  below,  and  provide  answers  to  the  research  questions  specified  in  the \nEvaluability Matrix included as Annex I to this document. As for the previous parameters, a summary \ngrading is reported in the Parameters Evaluation Matrix at the end of this chapter.44  \n\nAdequacy \n\nThe indicators defined in the CLECSAP logframe seem to be adequate to provide evidence on the \nresults  achieved  by  the  programme,  together  with  narrative  reporting  on  the  processes  and \nstrategies implemented.  \nMost  of  them  follow  a  horizontal  logic  that  links  the  activities  implemented  by  each  unit  to \nattributable outputs, and subsequently to outcomes.  \nOnly outcome indicator 2.2 (Number of social service workers per 100,000 child population) provides \nevidence that doesn’t seem to stem directly from the actions and the outputs  contributing to the \noutcome. The indicator measures in fact a result obtained through upstream policy advocacy work \nthat the COs involved are planning to do, but that is not documented in the results chain. The explicit \ninclusion  of  this  and  other  strategies  in  the  TOC  would  allow  to  highlight  the  strategic  reasoning \n\n43 As underlined by one of the members of the UNICEF SP and PMR teams interviewed in Pakistan (29th June \n2020; recording available)  \n44 For easy reference, the Indicator Definition and Monitoring Guide of the CLECSAP is included as Annex VI to \nthis document \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 18 OF 62 \n\n \n \n \n\x0cbehind the indicator, and to better include the evidence provided in an evaluation process. \nBy indicating a proportion, this indicator wouldn’t allow to measure quantitatively the outreach of \ndirect beneficiaries, which can be done only with one output indicator (3.1a). \nTwo indicators – outcome indicator 3 and output indicator 3.1b seem to focus on the same data for \nIndia  (districts  who  develop  community-based  model  of  care  and  reintegration;  districts  which \nimplement a multi-pronged strategy package).  \nMost  of  the  indicators  are  output  oriented,  and  therefore  are  fully  adequate  to  monitor  the \nimplementation progress. Considering the complexity of the programme, the declared intention to apply \nadaptive  learning  to  evidence  generated  through  data  analysis  and  studies,  and  lessons  drawn  from \nprogramme implementation,45 outcome and output indicators don’t seem to represent the main source \nof information required for adaptive decision making. More strategic exercises (such as the structured \nreflection meetings planned to be conducted during the programme implementation) will be needed. \n\nValidity \n\nAll the indicators are realistic in providing evidence on the specific actions implemented to achieve \nthe programme results, and evidently measurable. Baselines are easily verifiable – being focused on \nthe programme outputs, most of the indicators have a ‘zero’ baseline – and targets are in most of \nthe cases directly attributable to the CLECSAP interventions, and therefore realistic. The large part of \nindicators can be verified through sources and MoVs directly controlled by UNICEF, and therefore \neasily  documentable.  Some  indicators  would  require  the  collection  of  public  administrative  data, \nwhich seem  to  be  easily provided  by  Government  counterparts (since  the  issue  is  not  mentioned \neither  in  assumptions  or  in  risks).  The  disaggregation  proposed  for  most  indicators,  although \nproviding additional information, is often too simplistic and not related to the specific focus of the \nprogramme.  Data  is  disaggregated  for  the  purpose  of  revealing  underlying  trends,  patterns,  or \ninsights that would not be observable in aggregated data sets, for the full analytical evaluation of the \nresults achieved. If not contributing to this purpose, disaggregation is often irrelevant.  \nSection 4.5 contains detailed comments and suggestions on adjustments to the formulation of some \nindicators, to the level of ambition of some measurements, to the specifications of the sourcing and \nverification of data, and to types of disaggregation. Such amendments would contribute to highlight \nsome of the most valuable features of the programme, and increase the validity and readiness of the \nevidence provided for evaluation. \n\nCompleteness \n\nAll the indicators are thoroughly described in the definition and monitoring guide of the programme \ndocument. Baselines and targets are all defined, and only in one case there is no difference between \nthe baseline and the target.46 The responsible office should clarify why the indicator’s target is the \nsame as baseline throughout the programme. In a few cases, the level of ambition of the targets \ncould be increased or refined to better represent the value of the actions implemented to achieve \nthe results.47 \nAlmost  all  the  indicators  include  identified  geographic  locations,  specified  also  for  baselines  and \ntargets. A few additional information on the geographic scope of those indicators that don’t indicate \nit would facilitate the evaluation of the evidence reported. \nConsidering the nature of almost all the indicators, which refer to intellectual outputs, it is almost \nimpossible to define levels of disaggregation informed by equity and gender considerations. In two \ncases the indicators relate to the programme direct beneficiaries: the first one provides data on a \nratio  (social  service  workers  per  100,000  child  population),  and  therefore  the  disaggregation  by \ngender  and  disability  doesn’t  seem  to  be  particularly  relevant.  The  disaggregation  suggested  for \noutcome indicator 3 is de facto a separate indicator, focusing on the number and type of community \n\n45 Cf. UNICEF, Child labour; op. cit., page 24 \n46 Outcome indicator 2.1, for Pakistan \n47 Detailed recommendations on suggested adjustments are included in the matrix of section 4.5 \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 19 OF 62 \n\n \n \n \n\x0cgroups involved in the programme, while the indicator refers to the number of districts involved. \nAlthough  not  being  directly  related  to  the  indicator,  the  disaggregation  could  provide  relevant \ninformation; however, the discussion held during the validation meeting ascertained that one of the \ntwo COs involved wouldn’t most likely be in the condition to provide this type of information. \nThe  only  indicator  that  provides  quantitative data on  beneficiaries  is  output  indicator  3.1a, where \ndisaggregation is indicated by age and gender (although it is not very clear how this will be obtained). \nThe  proposed  disaggregation  by  “visible  disabilities”  would  need  to  be  reconsidered,  since  the \ncomputation  method  would  contribute  to  increase  the  misperceptions  and  stigma  related  to  the \nidentification of people with disability. A specific ethical safeguard is required in this case, as for all \nthe indicators that can be disaggregated by disability status. \n\nInformation management \n\nConsidering  the  type  of  sources  and  means  of  verification  required  for  the  indicators,  and  the \nmonitoring and reporting resources mobilized by UNICEF, there doesn’t seem to be  any particular \nconcern on the programme information management system. The type of data collected wouldn’t \nrequire a sophisticated level of analysis, and therefore programme staff involved would be in the \ncondition  to  access data,  monitor  change  and  manage  adjustments when required.  As  previously \nmentioned,  the  application  of  adaptive  learning  and  management would  require  more  structured \nexercises, and the involvement of all the programme key stakeholders. \n\nEquity \n\nAs  already  mentioned,  very  few  indicators  allow  for  a  disaggregation  which  would  substantiate \nequity considerations (focusing on gender, disabilities, and type of vulnerabilities). The segmentation \npossible for a few indicators (age and gender) doesn’t seem to provide relevant information for an \nevaluation of the equity-focused dimension of the programme. \nThis is mostly related to the fact that while the strategic approach and vision of the programme takes \nin primary consideration equity issues (focusing ultimately on the needs of a vulnerable category of \nchildren, and among those even more vulnerable groups, such as CWD and children on the move), \nindicators are not designed to provide evidence on the outreach of those indirect beneficiaries. \nWhile the CP team in Pakistan confirmed that it would be possible to gather administrative data on \nthe number of children outreached by the services and communities involved in the programme (also \nin consideration of the fact one objective of the programme is to set-up information management \nsystems tracking case management, as confirmed by one outcome indicator),48 both the teams in \nIndia and in the RO team raised concerns on including this type of indicator, in consideration of the \nrisk of not receiving the required data from the Government, and based on ethical reflections.49  \nThe same can be said in relation to an indicator measuring the number of children supported by inter-\nstate mechanisms for voluntary/safe return/repatriation (that would integrate Output indicator 2.3). \nAs  previously  mentioned,  the  only  case  in  which  a  disaggregation  by  disability  is  proposed  raises \nserious concerns on the type of assessment required (identify “visible” disabilities) and would need \nto  be  revised  to  avoid  stigmatization  and  violation  of  rights  of  PWD.  Besides,  this  segmentation \nwouldn’t in any case provide information on the focus of the programme, which is on CWD involved \nin child labour. \nNo  indicator  provides  evidence  of  a  specific  focus  on  gender  issues,  which  is  mentioned  in  the \nprogramme  document  as  a  strategy  implemented  throughout  the  engagement  with  service \nproviders and community members. It will be therefore very difficult to gather and evaluate evidence \non the gender transformative dimension of the programme, as recommended by UNICEF’s corporate \n\n48  Outcome  Indicator  2.2  –  Number  of  States  (India)  and  Provinces  (Pakistan)  with  integrated  information \nmanagement systems that support and track case management \n49  Cf.  in-depth  interviews  with  CP  teams  in  Nepal  (RO),  India  and  Pakistan  (24th  and  25th  June,  1st  July  2020; \nrecording available). As mentioned in footnote 37, the impossibility of gathering such type of administrative \ndata would need to be included in the risk assessment matrix, thus justifying the reason why it has been decided \nto avoid the risk \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 20 OF 62 \n\n \n \n \n\x0cguidance on evaluations. \nAs mentioned in the indicators’ description, and confirmed by several interviewees, gender and equity \nconsiderations will be applied to the review of all the programme intellectual outputs, including research \npapers, policy documents, and activity reports. However, this cannot be considered an adequate modality \nto implement or measure equity-focused dimensions to the results structure.  \n\n3.3 Parameters evaluation matrix  \n\nThe  considerations  reported  in  the  previous  sections  are  synthetized  in  the  matrix  below  as  a \nsummary grading of the level of readiness for evaluation of each parameter considered – for easy \nreference of the teams involved in the implementation of the programme and management decisions \non possible improvements of aspects that contribute to the programme evaluability. \nProposed  adjustments  to  results  statements  and  indicators,  aimed  at  addressing  the  weaknesses \nidentified, are detailed in the following chapter. \n\nEvaluability \nassessment \nobjectives \n\nAssess the \n\n1. \nclarity of outcomes, the \nTOC and the alignment of \nplanned programme \nefforts and strategies to \nthe TOC – as well as to the \nregional and country \ncontext, and UNICEF’s \nStrategic Plan \n\nInquiry \nparameter  \n\nClarity \n\nRelevance \n\nEffectiveness \n\nImpact \n\nCoherence \n\nTargeting  \n\nEquity \n\nRisks mitigation \n\nEvaluation \nreadiness  \n\nYES  Partial  NO \n\nSuggested areas of improvement50 \n\n  ✓ \n\n✓ \n\n  ✓ \n\n✓ \n\n  ✓ \n\n  ✓ \n\n  ✓ \n\n  ✓ \n\nAdjustments to the TOC to fully reflect the \nresults chain logic \n\nEmphasize convergence and intersectoral \nwork \n\nHighlight the links to the widely recommended \nprogrammatic strategies to reduce child labour  \n\nInclude geographic targeting in results \nstatements \n\nIdentify actions/strategies to enhance the \ngender transformative aspects of the \nprogramme \n\nInclude risk evaluation matrixes for each \nimplementing unit, and specify the type of \nresponses to risks \n\nContextualization  ✓ \n\nEfficiency \n\nSustainability \n\nAdequacy \n\n✓ \n✓ \n\nAssess the \n\n2. \nadequacy and validity of \nthe indicators, tools and \nsystems for monitoring, \nmeasuring and verifying \nresults. \n\nValidity \n\nCompleteness \n\n✓ \n\n✓ \n\n✓ \n\nHighlight the implementation of strategies \nsustaining the horizontal logic of one outcome \nindicator \n\nSpecify means of verification and \ndisaggregation of various indicators (detailed \nin the table of section 4.5) \n\nInclude geographical targeting where missing \n(and relevant) \n\n50 The summary grading and areas of improvement takes into consideration: the availability of sufficient data \nto  rate  the  evaluation  readiness  of  each  parameter;  the  evaluator’s  assessment  (based  on  evidence  from \nprimary and secondary data); the views of respondents on questions related to each parameter. More specific \nrecommendations related to the areas of improvement will be provided in Chapter 4 of this document \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 21 OF 62 \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\x0cEvaluability \nassessment \nobjectives \n\nInquiry \nparameter  \n\nEvaluation \nreadiness  \n\nYES  Partial  NO \n\nSuggested areas of improvement50 \n\nRevise disaggregation by disability  \n\nInformation \nmanagement \n\nEquity \n\n✓ \n\n✓ \n\nIdentify indicators where a disaggregation is \npossible and adequate to reflect the equity-\nfocused dimension of results  \n\n4. Recommendations \n\nThe  following  recommendations  are  logically  derived  from  the  findings  reported  in  the  previous \nchapter, and suggest possible ways forward to restructure the TOC, adjust the formulation of results, \nand  fine-tune  indicators  with  the  aim  of  increasing  the  evaluation  readiness  of  the  parameters \nassessed. This would contribute to strengthening the evaluability of the programme, harmonizing its \ncomponents  within  a  strategic  perspective,  and  enhancing  the  systemic  collection  of  relevant \nevidence. \n\n4.1 TOC  \n\nIn order to ensure the full alignment of the CLECSAP results logic to the TOC, and link the expected \nimpact of the programme to the specific problem addressed through a clear solution pathway, a few \nadjustments to the TOC are recommended, which gathered the consensus of the UNICEF COs teams \nconsulted: \n\n\uf0f0  The  problem  statement  should  reflect  the  specific  problem  addressed  by  programme, \nultimately related to the goal of reducing child labour. It should focus on the assessed low \ncapacity  of  services  and  communities  to  identify,  prevent,  and  respond  to  the  needs  of \neconomically exploited children. \n\n\uf0f0  The causal chain should directly link the problem statement to the bottlenecks included in \nthe TOC, which can replace the drivers and causes currently included, since they are largely \nnot going to be addressed by the CLECSAP.51 \n\n\uf0f0  The statements included in the TOC as strategies should rather be considered outputs; it is \nrecommended  to  include  all  the  programme  outputs  verbatim,  thus  ensuring  the  full \ncorrespondence of the logframe to the TOC.  \n\n\uf0f0  It  is  strongly  recommended  to  include  in  the  TOC,  below  the  outputs,  the  strategies \npromoted throughout the programme. These – and in particular: support for national and \ncommunity  ownership;  UNICEF  system-based  approach;  capacity  development;  policy \nadvocacy;  knowledge  management;  adaptive  approach  to  programme  –  are  the \nimplementation modalities that highlight UNICEF’s comparative advantage in implementing \nchild  protection  programmes,  and  point  out  the  connections  among  programme \ncomponents, increasing the overall clarity, effectiveness and coherence of the programme, \nand therefore its evaluability. The explicit inclusion of strategies in the TOC would allow to \ndocument  work  already  ongoing  but  not  fully  included  in  results  statements  and  activity \nplans, and provide additional evidence on aspects that add value to the programme. It is also \n\n51 While this is perfectly understandable from a strategic and realistic point of view, results based programme \nplanning guidance is very clear in underlying that “if a problem is caused by three conditions, addressing only \none or two conditions will not ensure that the problem is solved”. From an evaluability perspective, the causes \nunderlying a specific problem need to be all addressed by the interventions put in place to solve the problem. \nIncluding broader causes and drivers that would not be addressed is weakening from the beginning the results \nlogic, and undermining the programme evaluability. \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 22 OF 62 \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\x0crecommended to consider adding among the strategies a specific focus on gender equality \nand  inter-sectoral  cooperation,  thus  increasing  the  attention  on  these  two  key  strategic \naspects of the programme. \n\n\uf0f0  Outcomes  should  correspond  to  the  ones  included  in  the  programme  logframe,  and \n\npreferably be reported verbatim. \n\n\uf0f0  The impact statement is directly linked the specific problem addressed, but could be fine-\ntuned to become more evaluable - e.g.: (children in selected areas of India and Pakistan are \nbetter protected by)  effective systems and structures in place to prevent and respond to \nchild labour. \n\n\uf0f0  Assumptions should be revised to include all the conditions out of UNICEF control that are \n\nconsidered necessary to allow for the achievement of the planned results. \n\n4.2 Results framework  \n\nThe Logframe document doesn’t include the complete information required to monitor and evaluate \nthe  CLECSAP  results;  while  the  Indicator  Definition  and  Monitoring  Guide  includes,  together  with \ndetailed  indicators’  definitions,  the  methods  of  computation,  the  source/MoVs  of  indicators,  the \nlevels of disaggregation, and additional information. It is recommended therefore to consider this \ndocument as the Logframe, or to merge the monitoring guide and logframe into a comprehensive \nresults-monitoring framework.   \nOverall, results statements follow a logic that leads from a problem clearly identified in the programme \ndocument (but not in the TOC) to an impact statement indicating what is the expected change brought \nabout by the programme, through a quite clear sequence of activities, outputs and outcomes.   \nResults and indicators would require a few amendments to enhance their evaluability, based on the \nfindings reported above. The detailed comments and recommendations are included in the table of \nimplementing  unit’s  consideration.  Among  these,  the  most  relevant \nsection  4.5,  for  each \nrecommendations are the following: \n\nI.  Overall, it is recommended to add geographical targeting to outcome and impact statements, \n\nmaking them more specific and measurable.  \n\nII. \n\nIn consideration of actions reportedly ongoing that are not documented in results/indicators \nor in activity plans, and that deserve to be highlighted and contribute to the evaluability of \nthe programme, a few additional targets are recommended: (i) the documentation of the \nmodel  of  intervention  that  determines  the  high  ratio  of  social  service  workers  per  child \npopulation in Pakistan, representing a highly valuable good practice to be shared at national \nand  international  level,  should  be  added  to  output  indicator  1.1;52  (ii)  output  indicator  1.2 \nshould  increase  the  focus  on  KM  initiatives  aimed  at  further  promoting  the  circulation  of \nevidence and knowledge produced by the CLECSAP among all the stakeholders that could \ncontribute  to  the  improvement  of  interventions  to  reduce  child  labour  –  thus  enhancing \nmulti-sectoral convergence; (iii) indicator 3.1a could add a target also for India, considering \nthe awareness raising campaign already ongoing on social media and through community \nmobilization initiatives. \n\nIII. \n\nIn the case of outcome indicator 2.1, the horizontal logic leading from the activities to outputs \nand the outcome doesn’t provide evidence for the adequacy of the indicator. In this case, the \nexplicit reference to the strategic approach applied to achieve the result measured by the \nindicator (policy advocacy) will allow to justify the selection of this indicator and increase the \nevaluability of the programme component.  \n\nIV.  Overall, it is necessary to re-think the MoVs and define more specifically the sourcing and \ntiming of data, adding where possible official and verifiable reports to UNICEF’s ones. \n\n52  If  the  recommendation  is  not  accepted,  it  is  suggested  to  identify  other  opportunities  to  document  the \npractice \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 23 OF 62 \n\n \n \n \n\x0cV. \n\nVI. \n\nThe levels of disaggregation of indicators are in general quite simplistic, and in many cases \nnot relevant to the objectives of the programme. Wherever possible it is suggested to refine \nor  rethink  them,  especially  in  light  of  the  considerations  previously  reported  on  the \nevaluation readiness of the equity parameter.  Among the possible revisions, the following \nare suggested:53 outcome indicator 2.1 can include a disaggregation by disability (although \nnot specifically related to the programme objectives); output indicator 2.2 can be integrated \nwith an additional  indicator measuring the functionality of case management mechanisms \nthrough the number of children reached (and documented through the IMS)  – this would \nallow a meaningful disaggregation by gender, disability status, and type of vulnerability; the \ndisaggregation  methodology  of  indicator  3.1a  needs  to  be  revised  to  provide  for  an \nacceptable and realistic estimation of the number of PWD involved.  \n\nLast but not least, there is very little evidence on strategies and actions aimed at enhancing \nthe  gender  transformative  approach  of  the  programme,  and  no  reflection  of  these  in \nindicators. Together with ensuring the integration of a gender dimension into the research \nmethodology  of  the  in-depth  analysis  of  child  labour  and  CWD,  a  dedicated  reflection  is \nrecommended to identify in the programme design and in the results framework entry points \nto  ensure  an  increased  focus  on  gender  equality  issues,  in  line  with  UNICEF’s  corporate \npriorities.54 \n\n4.3 Programme implementation and governance \n\nI.    As  already  mentioned,  and  repeatedly  discussed  with  the  stakeholders  interviewed,  the  most \nrelevant  and  noteworthy  feature  of  CLECSAP  is  the  convergence  of  various  public  policies, \nprogrammes and services to enhance an integrated, systemic response to child labour.  \nExpanding  to  its  full  potential  the  model  of  integrated  social  services  promoted  at  service \nprovision level would allow to give value to the inter-sectoral work reportedly already ongoing \nand  increase  the  coherence  and  effectiveness  of  the  programme,  by  realizing  a  more  holistic \napproach.55  \nThis can be done in particular through the expansion of the KM component of the programme, \nincluded in the actions related to outcome 1. Knowledge brokering is one of the key strategies of \nthe CLECSAP; being an area where UNICEF can clearly build on its comparative advantage, the \nstrategy  can  be  further  expanded  to:  (a)  multiply  the  positive  effect  of  knowledge  exchanges \namong  duty  bearers  at  national  and  international  level,  through  the  documentation  of  good \npractices identified by the project; (b) foster inter-sectoral cooperation within UNICEF and among \ncounterparts,  and  increase  the  programme  effectiveness  by  providing  evidence  to  sustain \nupstream  strategic  interventions  contributing  to  the  elimination  of  child  labour,  namely  social \nprotection and education. As mentioned in the recent Knowledge Review prepared by the CP unit \nin  UNICEF  ROSA,  “KM  is  not  an  end,  it  is  a  means  to  improve  the  relevance,  effectiveness, \nefficiency  and  sustainability  of  results  achieved  by  UNICEF  on  behalf  of  children.  (…)  This \n[regional] framework makes a difference between, on one hand, the management of knowledge \nfor internal learning purposes and, on the other, the use of knowledge for advocacy, upstream \npolicy  work,  joint  research/evaluation  or  partners’  capacity  building  for  effective  knowledge \nmanagement”.56 In this sense, it is recommended to shift the focus of the technical assistance \nprovided  more  decisively  towards  structured  KM  and  reflection  meetings,  engaging  key \n\n53 Other possible recommendations have not been included in consideration of specific constraints discussed \nduring in-depth interviews and at the validation meeting \n54  Cf.,  among  other  documents,  UNICEF’s  Gender  Action  Plan  2018-2021:  https://www.unicef.org/gender-\nequality/gender-action-plan-2018-2021 \n55 This approach is more explicit in the description of activities and mode of delivery of outcome 3, especially \nfor India. Cf. UNICEF, Child Labour:, op. cit., page 22 \n56 UNICEF ROSA, Knowledge Review, submitted by Ritu Kumar, May 2020, page 4 \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 24 OF 62 \n\n \n \n \n \n\x0cstakeholders at international level; organise at least one inter-sectoral event in each country to \npromote the application of the new knowledge to policy and programme decisions contributing \nto the elimination of child labour; make UNICEF’s internal inter-sectoral cooperation more visible, \nby sharing knowledge that can trigger upstream policy advocacy initiatives in other sectors (e.g., \nthe new data on children with disabilities and labour can be used to enhance inclusive education \nand targeted social protection). An increased focus on KM would also align with two priority areas \nidentified  for  the  KM  Regional  Strategy  for  Child  Protection,  child  marriage  and  disability: \nopportunities  for  collaboration  (integrated  KM  approaches,  cross  sectoral  knowledge  events, \nonline  communities)  and  staff  as  knowledge  assets.57  Team  members \nin  the \nimplementation  of  CLECSAP  can  become  KM  champions  on  child  labour,  and  share  acquired \nknowledge and expertise with colleagues in the region and beyond. As repeatedly mentioned in \nthe Knowledge Review, people are the single most important component of KM. \n\ninvolved \n\nII.  In order to ensure the application of UNICEF’s enterprise risk management principles, it is strongly \nrecommended to develop one risk assessment and control matrix for each implementing unit; the \nidentified risks will have to be rated in terms of likelihood and impact, and the unit decision on \nhow to respond to those will have to be documented. This will also allow to justify some choices \nrelated to results and indicators. \n\nIII.  Governance  mechanisms  seem  to  be  in  place  for  the  regular  data  collection  and  monitoring \nrelated to the programme, both in terms of human and financial resources. It is recommended to \nmaintain a constant focus on the M&E and KM aspects of the programme, especially in relation to \nthe  collection  of  administrative  data  from  Government  counterparts,  which  will  have  to  be \nspecifically sourced and with defined timeframes. \n\nIV. Despite its fully participatory approach, the EA exercise has not involved directly the ultimate, \nindirect beneficiaries of the programme – i.e. children involved in child labour – since the type of \nconsultation wouldn’t be suitable for them. However, it is very important to provide within the \nprogramme for a safe space where children’s voices can be heard. As repeatedly underlined by \nthe Committee on the Rights of the Child, children have a right to voice their views on matters \naffecting  them.58  One  or  more  consultations  with  children  throughout  the  programme \nimplementation will also substantiate the application of the human rights-based approach, which \n“recognizes children and adolescents involved in or at risk of child labour as key actors in their \nown development, instead of passive recipient of benefits”.59 These could be organised as part of \nthe  structured  reflection  meetings  to  review  the  project  progress  and  discuss  innovative \npractices, described in Output 1.2. \n\n4.4 Resources allocation \n\nSince  all  the  recommended  changes  and  enhancements  to  the  programme  are  focusing  on \nhighlighting the value of not explicit actions already ongoing, there doesn’t seem to be a particular \nneed for additional resources allocation. \nAs already mentioned, especially for Pakistan the budget available is only sufficient to cover for the \npartial realization of the planned actions, and results will be achieved thanks to the  integration of \n\n57 Cf. UNICEF ROSA & UNFPA APRO, Knowledge Management Strategy: HOW DO WE GET THERE, final draft, July \n2017, page 2 \n58 Cf. Committee on the Rights of the Child, General Comment No. 12 (2009) The right of the child to be heard, \nCRC/C/GC/12, 1 July 2009 \n59 UNICEF, Child labour: op. cit., page 26. The TOC also includes, among the outcomes, the following statement: \n“Children who are at risk and/or economically exploited, including child labourers affected by disability and/or \non the move, are able to make choices and access relevant support services”. If the recommendation to report \nverbatim the outcomes included in the logframe is accepted, this result will have to be deleted. In case the \ndecision  is  to  maintain  it,  a  couple  of  revisions  are  suggested:  replace  “affected  by”  disability  with  “with \ndisability”, and reconsider maintaining the reference to ‘choices’. Although children can actively participate in \ndecisions related to their lives, the fact that they can make choices that could avoid their involvement in child \nlabour, and in particular their economic exploitation, is very debatable. \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 25 OF 62 \n\n \n \n \n\x0cother resources, and therefore will not be fully attributable to the CLECSAP funding. \nThe  additional  actions recommended  as  part  of  the  KM work  can  be sustained with  little  UNICEF \nfunds, as confirmed by the CO teams consulted. \nNew considerations are probably required in relation to the impact of the CoVid19 pandemic on the \nsocio-economic situation of the countries involved in the programme, on public financial constraints, \nand on the expected increase in the involvement of children in labour and economic exploitation. \nOverall, the CLECSAP seems to be already designed to respond to the changed situation. However, \nshould part of the funds be diverted upon the donor’s request for the CoVid19 emergency response, \nthe implications for the implementation of the CLECSAP will have to be considered. \nSince  a  solid  budget  line  has  been  already  created  for  the  programme  evaluation,  the  options \nincluded in section 4.6 don’t seem to suggest the need for additional resources.  \n\nCLECSAP EVALUABILITY ASSESSMENT - FINAL REPORT \n\nPAGE 26 OF 62 \n\n \n \n \n\x0c4.5 De