t time we \nreceive a BIT \nvisit  at the \nSUDES HQ”  \n\nBIT – \nEmployers \nActivities \n\nHigh degree \n\nOnly contact at \nmeetings \n\nimpact. \n\nOnly some \nindirect impact on \nthe working \napproach, but little \nconcrete lasting \nimpact  \n\nThrough \nexperience in \nLomé Workshop, \nSUDES is trying a \nsaving-and-credit \nscheme through \nits Farmers Union \nmember.  Besides \nsome further \nprofessional \nenrichment, no \nother impact \n\nThe impact of the \nproject has only \nremained “at the \ntop”.  No concrete \nefforts have been \nundertaken to put \nthe knowledge \nand ideas into \nconcrete actions.    \n\nN/A \n\nN/A \n\nThrough ToR of this \nEvaluation, in \nCasablanca it was \nunderstood there \nwould be a new \nproject phase.   \n\nThe project: \n“When the ILO \ninvites us, \nnobody in \nparticular feels \nconcerned “ and  \n“We had just a \ntaste of \nsomething good, \nbut unachieved!”  \n\nThe project has \nnot really \nintegrated \nSUDES in its \nactivities, it has \nbeen more the \nopposite… \n\nBecause of the \nserious \nexportation of \ncompetent labor \nfrom the South \nto the North, the \nBIT should \ncontinue to be \ninvolved and \nsensitize the \nemployers on \nthe following \nthree points: \n\n1.As tripartite \npartner within \nBIT they should \nactively adhere \nto Conventions. \n\n2.Labor \nmigration \nrepresents an \nenormous loss \nof competence. \n\n3.The returnees \nbring a lot of \nintellectual and \n\n52\n\n \n\x0cOrganisation \n\nDegree of \nknowledge \non project \n\nDegree of \ncollaboration \nwith project \n\nEstimated \nimpact of project \n\nUnderstanding of \n“end project” \n\nMessage to ILO \n\nfinancial \nresources, which \nshould be well \nexploited for the \neconomy. \n\n4.Migrants within \nthe countries \nneed to be \nknown and \nbetter utilized \nwithin the \nnational labor \nmarket. \n\nUnderstanding of \n“end project” \n\nMessage to ILO \n\nFirst evaluation and \nthen possible follow \nup.  The partners \nhave